{"notes":[{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["role representation","prompt injection","instruction hierarchy","security"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/2c7a3333c3c0cf6c3f4b952a475148b21f00ae74.zip"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nye2026prompt,\ntitle={Prompt Injection as Role Confusion},\nauthor={Charles Ye and Jasmine Cui and Dylan Hadfield-Menell},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Lm3DQDyIsI}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Prompt Injection as Role Confusion"},"paperhash":{"value":"ye|prompt_injection_as_role_confusion"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/0458c58420f1c98f1b3e6e9fcc530e3ec3442258.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"We trace prompt injection to role confusion: models infer roles from style, not tags, and we introduce tools to measure this."},"primary_area":{"value":"social_aspects->accountability_transparency_and_interpretability"},"abstract":{"value":"LLMs see the world as a single stream of text, partitioned into *roles* like &lt;user&gt; or &lt;tool&gt;. We trace **prompt injection** to **role confusion**: models perceive the source of text from *how it sounds*, not its labeled role. A command hidden in a webpage hijacks an agent simply because it sounds like &lt;user&gt; text, despite its &lt;tool&gt; label. We design *role probes* to measure how LLMs internally perceive \"who is speaking\", and find that injected text occupies the same representational space as the trusted role it imitates. We demonstrate this with CoT Forgery, a zero-shot attack that injects fabricated reasoning into user prompts and tool outputs. Models mistake the forgery for their own thoughts, yielding 60% attack success against frontier models with near-zero baselines. Strikingly, the degree of role confusion predicts attack success before a single token is generated. This mechanism generalizes beyond CoT Forgery to standard agent prompt injections, revealing prompt injection as a measurable consequence of role perception. To the model, sounding like a role is indistinguishable from being one."},"link_to_code":{"value":"https://role-confusion.github.io/"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8840ad94b31fe89a62437eeeb8d05a933a5bc5aa.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"AI assistants follow instructions from different sources: developers set rules, users ask questions, and tools fetch external data like webpages. To stay safe, the AI needs to tell these sources apart. If it can’t, a malicious instruction hidden in a webpage could be mistaken for a legitimate command. Current defenses against such attacks fail routinely against determined adversaries, but the reason has been unclear.\nWe discovered that AI models identify “who is speaking” based on how text sounds, not where it actually comes from. A command hidden in a webpage can hijack an AI agent simply because it sounds like a user's instruction, a failure we call \"role confusion\". To demonstrate it, we injected fake reasoning into AI prompts. Models misattribute the source, believing this fake reasoning to be their own thoughts, leading to harmful behaviors.\nWe built tools to look inside the model, finding that prompt injections in general succeed when AIs misattribute the source of text. The greater the misattribution, the more likely a prompt injection succeeds. These findings reveal a structural flaw in how AI models process text, and our framework provides tools to measure and address it."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Charles_Ye2","~Jasmine_Cui2","~Dylan_Hadfield-Menell2"]},"authors":{"value":["Charles Ye","Jasmine Cui","Dylan Hadfield-Menell"]}},"tmdate":1790067444363,"pdate":1777576938032,"tcdate":1769239106928,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission32288/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission32288/Authors"],"forum":"Lm3DQDyIsI","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":32288,"cdate":1769239106928,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission32288/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission32288/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067444363,"odate":1782342036940,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"Lm3DQDyIsI","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SP 2026"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"jia|promptlocate_localizing_prompt_injection_attacks"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SP63933.2026.00105"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/sp/JiaLSJG26,\n  author={Yuqi Jia and Yupei Liu and Zedian Shao and Jinyuan Jia and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={PromptLocate: Localizing Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  pages={4243-4261},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/SP63933.2026.00105},\n  booktitle={SP},\n  crossref={conf/sp/2026}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"PromptLocate: Localizing Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuqi Jia","username":"~Yuqi_Jia1"},{"fullname":"Yupei Liu","username":""},{"fullname":"Zedian Shao","username":""},{"fullname":"Jinyuan Jia","username":""},{"fullname":"Neil Zhenqiang Gong","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785854938319,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/sp/JiaLSJG26"],"tcdate":1785854937609,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yuqi_Jia1"],"forum":"ulwRfUOxb3","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":115807,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785854938319,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"ulwRfUOxb3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.12252v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"jia|promptlocate_localizing_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuqi_Jia1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yupei_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zedian_Shao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyuan_Jia_0001:","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.12252"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2510-12252,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuqi Jia and Yupei Liu and Zedian Shao and Jinyuan Jia and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={PromptLocate: Localizing Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2025},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1759276800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2510.12252},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.12252}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection attacks deceive a large language model into completing an attacker-specified task instead of its intended task by contaminating its input data with an injected prompt, which consists of injected instruction(s) and data. Localizing the injected prompt within contaminated data is crucial for post-attack forensic analysis and data recovery. Despite its growing importance, prompt injection localization remains largely unexplored. In this work, we bridge this gap by proposing PromptLocate, the first method for localizing injected prompts. PromptLocate comprises three steps: (1) splitting the contaminated data into semantically coherent segments, (2) identifying segments contaminated by injected instructions, and (3) pinpointing segments contaminated by injected data. We show PromptLocate accurately localizes injected prompts across eight existing and eight adaptive attacks."},"title":{"value":"PromptLocate: Localizing Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Yuqi Jia","Yupei Liu","Zedian Shao","Jinyuan Jia","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1785854938174,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2510-12252"],"tcdate":1764166585323,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"0KIPtn084H","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706404,"cdate":1759276800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785854938174,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0KIPtn084H","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.14964v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"kujanpää|knowledge_injection_via_prompt_distillation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kalle_Kujanpää:","~Harri_Valpola2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Alexander_Ilin:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.14964"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2412-14964,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Kalle Kujanpää and Harri Valpola and Alexander Ilin},\n  title={Knowledge Injection via Prompt Distillation},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2412.14964},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.14964}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"In many practical applications, large language models (LLMs) need to acquire new knowledge not present in their pre-training data. Efficiently leveraging this knowledge usually relies on supervised fine-tuning or retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Although RAG has emerged as the industry standard for knowledge injection, fine-tuning has not yet achieved comparable success. This paper proposes utilizing prompt distillation, a self-distillation-based method previously explored primarily for style alignment and instruction tuning, to internalize new factual knowledge from free-form documents. Unlike prior methods, our approach requires neither larger teacher models nor structured knowledge formats. Across multiple LLM sizes and model families, we show that prompt distillation outperforms standard supervised fine-tuning and can even surpass RAG. We analyze the key factors contributing to prompt distillation's effectiveness and examine how it scales."},"title":{"value":"Knowledge Injection via Prompt Distillation"},"authors":{"value":["Kalle Kujanpää","Harri Valpola","Alexander Ilin"]}},"tmdate":1769330078105,"pdate":1735603200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2412-14964"],"tcdate":1769330041315,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Harri_Valpola2"],"forum":"XFpaEQOsIj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":795875,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769330078105,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"XFpaEQOsIj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models","Fingerprinting","LLM Provenance Detection"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) are often modified after release through post-processing such as post-training or quantization, which makes it challenging to determine whether one model is derived from another. Existing provenance detection methods have two main limitations: (1) they embed signals into the base model before release, which is infeasible for already published models, or (2) they compare outputs across models using hand-crafted or random prompts, which are not robust to post-processing. In this work, we propose LLMPrint, a novel detection framework that constructs fingerprints by exploiting LLMs’ inherent vulnerability to prompt injection. Our key insight is that by optimizing fingerprint prompts to enforce consistent token preferences, we can obtain fingerprints that are both unique to the base model and robust to post-processing. We further develop a unified verification procedure that applies to both gray-box and black-box settings, with statistical guarantees. We evaluate LLMPrint on five base models and around 700 post-trained or quantized variants. Our results show that LLMPrint achieves high true positive rates while keeping false positive rates near zero."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nhu2025fingerprinting,\ntitle={Fingerprinting {LLM}s via Prompt Injection},\nauthor={Yuepeng Hu and Zhengyuan Jiang and Mengyuan Li and Osama Ahmed and Zhicong Huang and Cheng Hong and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ND0q3wjNgW}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Fingerprinting LLMs via Prompt Injection"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2b7ebf54cdde83bd0d88c42075f7afdaece6d985.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|fingerprinting_llms_via_prompt_injection"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuepeng_Hu1","~Zhengyuan_Jiang1","~Mengyuan_Li6","~Osama_Ahmed2","~Zhicong_Huang2","~Cheng_Hong1","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"authors":{"value":["Yuepeng Hu","Zhengyuan Jiang","Mengyuan Li","Osama Ahmed","Zhicong Huang","Cheng Hong","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1764691055314,"tcdate":1758214272591,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission13158/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission13158/Authors"],"forum":"ND0q3wjNgW","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":13158,"cdate":1758214272591,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission13158/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1764691055314,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"ND0q3wjNgW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.25448v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"hu|fingerprinting_llms_via_prompt_injection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuepeng_Hu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhengyuan_Jiang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mengyuan_Li:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Osama_Ahmed:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhicong_Huang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Cheng_Hong_0001:","~Neil_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.25448"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2509-25448,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuepeng Hu and Zhengyuan Jiang and Mengyuan Li and Osama Ahmed and Zhicong Huang and Cheng Hong and Neil Gong},\n  title={Fingerprinting LLMs via Prompt Injection},\n  year={2025},\n  month={September},\n  cdate={1756684800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2509.25448},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.25448}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) are often modified after release through post-processing such as post-training or quantization, which makes it challenging to determine whether one model is derived from another. Existing provenance detection methods have two main limitations: (1) they embed signals into the base model before release, which is infeasible for already published models, or (2) they compare outputs across models using hand-crafted or random prompts, which are not robust to post-processing. In this work, we propose LLMPrint, a novel detection framework that constructs fingerprints by exploiting LLMs' inherent vulnerability to prompt injection. Our key insight is that by optimizing fingerprint prompts to enforce consistent token preferences, we can obtain fingerprints that are both unique to the base model and robust to post-processing. We further develop a unified verification procedure that applies to both gray-box and black-box settings, with statistical guarantees. We evaluate LLMPrint on five base models and around 700 post-trained or quantized variants. Our results show that LLMPrint achieves high true positive rates while keeping false positive rates near zero."},"title":{"value":"Fingerprinting LLMs via Prompt Injection"},"authors":{"value":["Yuepeng Hu","Zhengyuan Jiang","Mengyuan Li","Osama Ahmed","Zhicong Huang","Cheng Hong","Neil Gong"]}},"tmdate":1764166607317,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2509-25448"],"tcdate":1764166585366,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"6WOGaBxcAW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706406,"cdate":1756684800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1764166607317,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"6WOGaBxcAW","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Inference time defense method to guard Large Language Models against adversarial attacks"},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Safety","Alignment","Defense","Adversarial Attack","Inference","LLM","NLP"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used in a variety of important applications, yet their safety and reliability remain major concerns. Various adversarial and jailbreak attacks have been proposed to bypass the safety alignment and cause the model to produce harmful responses. We introduce Defensive Self-supervised Prompt INjection (D-SPIN) which can detect and reverse these various attacks on LLMs. Just by injecting an adaptive defense prompt at inference-time, our method is simple, effective, and compatible with existing safety-aligned models. Our benchmarks demonstrate that our system can reduce the attack success rate by up to 87.9\\%, while maintaining the performance on benign user requests. In addition, we discuss the situation of an adaptive attacker and show that our method is still resilient against attackers who are aware of our defense."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nzhou2025spin,\ntitle={{SPIN}: Self-Supervised Prompt {IN}jection},\nauthor={Leon Zhou and Junfeng Yang and Chengzhi Mao},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=PNHGYziAsL}\n}"},"title":{"value":"SPIN: Self-Supervised Prompt INjection"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3a22688bc4e5a8685a2964d2ea7b52cd1c029b89.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|spin_selfsupervised_prompt_injection"},"authorids":{"value":["~Leon_Zhou1","~Junfeng_Yang1","~Chengzhi_Mao2"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Leon Zhou","Junfeng Yang","Chengzhi Mao"]}},"tmdate":1738735618744,"tcdate":1726266968359,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission577/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission577/Authors"],"forum":"PNHGYziAsL","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":577,"cdate":1726266968359,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission577/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission577/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738735618744,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"PNHGYziAsL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 October Submission"},"research_area_keywords":{"value":"probing, data shortcuts/artifacts, robustness, adversarial attacks/examples/training, evaluation methodologies, reproducibility, tool use, agent evaluation"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM Agents","Prompt Injection","Interpretability"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026promptinjection,\ntitle={Prompt-Injection Probes Learn the Conversation, Not Just the Injection},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - October 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=0YISlkuO5d},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Prompt-Injection Probes Learn the Conversation, Not Just the Injection"},"languages_studied":{"value":["eng"]},"contribution_types":{"value":"Model analysis & interpretability"},"abstract":{"value":"Linear probes on a model's hidden states are used as cheap monitors for indirect prompt injection: they screen the tool outputs an agent reads and send suspicious ones to a slower second check, so every false alarm has a cost. But a probe learns whatever separates its training examples. The standard evaluation of injection probes can be passed without the model: conversation position and text lengths alone reach 0.91--0.98 AUROC. When each attack is compared with the same conversation without the injection, or with harmless text formatted like an injection, standard probes flag most of these harmless cases. We add such cases to the training data as \\emph{matched negatives}. Across six open models, in applications seen during training and at the default threshold, changing only the training data cuts false alarms on injection-free conversations from 41--80\\% to 4--27\\%, and on harmless look-alikes from 92--98\\% to 3--16\\%. With a threshold set on normal traffic, our probe still catches a median 95\\% of the attacks that fooled the agent (91\\% without our data). In new applications, false alarms stay low, but detection depends on the model and on how the threshold is set. The same data also helps a text-only filter."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/667511df47464b79cb6ca8aff79d431fe9166ab5.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission"}},"tmdate":1792184984032,"tcdate":1790803288059,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission441/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission441/Authors"],"forum":"0YISlkuO5d","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":441,"cdate":1790803288059,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Responsible_NLP_Checklist_Status","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission441/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1792184984032,"odate":1791894541772,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","id":"0YISlkuO5d","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.13169v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"mchugh|prompt_injection_20_hybrid_ai_threats"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.13169"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-13169,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jeremy McHugh and Kristina Sekrst and Jonathan Rodriguez Cefalu},\n  title={Prompt Injection 2.0: Hybrid AI Threats},\n  year={2025},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1751328000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2507.13169},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.13169}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection attacks, where malicious input is designed to manipulate AI systems into ignoring their original instructions and following unauthorized commands instead, were first discovered by Preamble, Inc. in May 2022 and responsibly disclosed to OpenAI. Over the last three years, these attacks have continued to pose a critical security threat to LLM-integrated systems. The emergence of agentic AI systems, where LLMs autonomously perform multistep tasks through tools and coordination with other agents, has fundamentally transformed the threat landscape. Modern prompt injection attacks can now combine with traditional cybersecurity exploits to create hybrid threats that systematically evade traditional security controls. This paper presents a comprehensive analysis of Prompt Injection 2.0, examining how prompt injections integrate with Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF), and other web security vulnerabilities to bypass traditional security measures. We build upon Preamble's foundational research and mitigation technologies, evaluating them against contemporary threats, including AI worms, multi-agent infections, and hybrid cyber-AI attacks. Our analysis incorporates recent benchmarks that demonstrate how traditional web application firewalls, XSS filters, and CSRF tokens fail against AI-enhanced attacks. We also present architectural solutions that combine prompt isolation, runtime security, and privilege separation with novel threat detection capabilities."},"title":{"value":"Prompt Injection 2.0: Hybrid AI Threats"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Jeremy McHugh","username":""},{"fullname":"Kristina Sekrst","username":"~Kristina_Šekrst1"},{"fullname":"Jonathan Rodriguez Cefalu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785845535546,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2507-13169"],"tcdate":1785845532202,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Kristina_Šekrst1"],"forum":"B9oy98PoXE","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":114894,"cdate":1751328000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785845535546,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"B9oy98PoXE","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"The textual collaborative prompt optimization reveals a new attack surface for prompt injection risks."},"venue":{"value":"COLM 2026"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the COLM Code of Ethics on https://colmweb.org/CoE.html"},"LLM_usage":{"value":"In accordance with the Policy on the use of Large Language Models in the Call for Papers https://colmweb.org/cfp.html, I certify that my submission discloses any substantive use of LLMs in the research and paper-writing process."},"keywords":{"value":["Federated learning","Prompt Injection Attack","Prompt Optimization"]},"abstract":{"value":"Textual Collaborative Prompt Optimization (TCPO) extends TextGrad (Yuksekgonul et al., 2025) to a decentralized setting by allowing multiple clients to jointly improve prompts for large language models (LLMs) while keeping their data locally. Its reliance on free-form textual updating and aggregation introduces a new and largely unexplored attack surface, i.e., malicious instructions can be injected into local prompts and propagated through server-side prompt aggregation. Unlike conventional prompt injection attacks, attacking TCPO targets the collaborative optimization loop in TCPO. This setting is more challenging because malicious instructions must survive aggregation, persist through subsequent benign prompt optimization, and evade server-side defenses. To expose this risk, we propose Collaborative Prompt Injection (CPInj) attack that contaminates the aggregated global prompt with malicious instructions, degrades downstream task performance, resists purification by prompt optimization on benign clients, and evades advanced detection-based defenses on the server. We find that current defense methods are ineffective against CPInj. We further propose Anchored Purification Aggregation (APAgg), a defense-oriented aggregation that purifies malicious instructions without severely degrading TCPO utility. We conduct extensive experiments across three LLM families and five reasoning tasks in math, logic, and medicine, and demonstrate that our proposed attack reveals a critical vulnerability in TCPO. Although we take a first step toward mitigation, the attack remains highly effective and far from fully resolved, calling for more robust defense for TCPO."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nliao2026cpinj,\ntitle={{CPI}nj: Uncovering Prompt Injection Risks in Textual Collaborative Prompt Optimization},\nauthor={Xinting Liao and Behnoosh Zamanlooy and Masoumeh Shafieinejad and D. B. Emerson and Ruinan Jin and Deval Pandya and Xiaoxiao Li},\nbooktitle={Third Conference on Language Modeling},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=YVzz7CATwd}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CPInj: Uncovering Prompt Injection Risks in Textual Collaborative Prompt Optimization"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d4a33dfab341e782e63d44030471588ed625b04b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"liao|cpinj_uncovering_prompt_injection_risks_in_textual_collaborative_prompt_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xinting_Liao1","~Behnoosh_Zamanlooy2","~Masoumeh_Shafieinejad1","~D._B._Emerson1","~Ruinan_Jin4","~Deval_Pandya1","~Xiaoxiao_Li1"]},"author_guide":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://colmweb.org/AuthorGuide.html"},"authors":{"value":["Xinting Liao","Behnoosh Zamanlooy","Masoumeh Shafieinejad","D. B. Emerson","Ruinan Jin","Deval Pandya","Xiaoxiao Li"]}},"tmdate":1787537156803,"pdate":1783543361744,"tcdate":1774075118226,"writers":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission357/Authors"],"signatures":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission357/Authors"],"forum":"YVzz7CATwd","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":357,"cdate":1774075118226,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission357/-/Full_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Edit","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission357/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1787537156803,"odate":1787537156569,"domain":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","id":"YVzz7CATwd","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"TLDR":{"value":"The textual collaborative prompt optimization reveals a new attack surface for prompt injection risks."},"venue":{"value":"AI4GOOD Workshop 2026 Regular"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["Federated learning","Prompt Injection Attack","Prompt Optimization"]},"abstract":{"value":"Textual Collaborative Prompt Optimization (TCPO) extends TextGrad (Yuksekgonul et al., 2025) to a decentralized setting by allowing multiple clients to jointly improve prompts for large language models (LLMs) while keeping their data locally. Its reliance on free-form textual updating and aggregation introduces a new and largely unexplored attack surface, i.e., malicious instructions can be injected into local prompts and propagated through server-side prompt aggregation. Unlike conventional prompt injection attacks, attacking TCPO targets the collaborative optimization loop in TCPO. This setting is more challenging because malicious instructions must survive aggregation, persist through subsequent benign prompt optimization, and evade server-side defenses. To expose this risk, we propose Collaborative Prompt Injection (CPInj) attack that contaminates the aggregated global prompt with malicious instructions, degrades downstream task performance, resists purification by prompt optimization on benign clients, and evades advanced detection-based defenses on the server. We find that current defense methods are ineffective against CPInj. We further propose Anchored Purification Aggregation (APAgg), a defense-oriented aggregation that purifies malicious instructions without severely degrading TCPO utility. We conduct extensive experiments across three LLM families and five reasoning tasks in math, logic, and medicine, and demonstrate that our proposed attack reveals a critical vulnerability in TCPO. Although we take a first step toward mitigation, the attack remains highly effective and far from fully resolved, calling for more robust defense for TCPO."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nliao2026cpinj,\ntitle={{CPI}nj: Uncovering Prompt Injection Risks in Textual Collaborative Prompt Optimization},\nauthor={Xinting Liao and Behnoosh Zamanlooy and Masoumeh Shafieinejad and D. B. Emerson and Ruinan Jin and Deval Pandya and Xiaoxiao Li},\nbooktitle={Trustworthy AI for Good (AI4GOOD) Workshop @ ICML 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=fQqNvvyxKA}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CPInj: Uncovering Prompt Injection Risks in Textual Collaborative Prompt Optimization"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/11b4d46dc04a6a1378e33cdae1ca2e5876be0efe.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD"},"paperhash":{"value":"liao|cpinj_uncovering_prompt_injection_risks_in_textual_collaborative_prompt_optimization"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xinting_Liao1","~Behnoosh_Zamanlooy2","~Masoumeh_Shafieinejad1","~D._B._Emerson1","~Ruinan_Jin4","~Deval_Pandya1","~Xiaoxiao_Li1"]},"authors":{"value":["Xinting Liao","Behnoosh Zamanlooy","Masoumeh Shafieinejad","D. B. Emerson","Ruinan Jin","Deval Pandya","Xiaoxiao Li"]}},"tmdate":1785452642611,"pdate":1780485952343,"tcdate":1776646355141,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/Submission27/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/Submission27/Authors"],"forum":"fQqNvvyxKA","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":27,"cdate":1776646355141,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/-/Submission_Release","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/Submission27/-/Camera_Ready_Revision","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785452642611,"odate":1780485952343,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AI4GOOD","id":"fQqNvvyxKA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 October Submission"},"research_area_keywords":{"value":"LLM agents, security, robustness, prompt injection"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM agent","security","robustness","prompt injection"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026agentwatcher,\ntitle={AgentWatcher: A Rule-based Prompt Injection Monitor},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - October 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=NNbyBLNGSa},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"AgentWatcher: A Rule-based Prompt Injection Monitor"},"contribution_types":{"value":"Model analysis & interpretability"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) and their applications, such as agents, are highly vulnerable to prompt injection attacks. State-of-the-art prompt injection detection methods have the following limitations: (1) their effectiveness degrades significantly as context length increases, and (2) they lack explicit rules that define what constitutes prompt injection, causing detection decisions to be implicit, opaque, and difficult to reason about. In this work, we propose AgentWatcher to address the above two limitations. To address the first limitation, AgentWatcher attributes the LLM's output (e.g., the action of an agent) to a small set of causally influential context segments. By focusing detection on a relatively short text, AgentWatcher can be scalable to long contexts. To address the second limitation, we define a set of rules specifying what does and does not constitute a prompt injection, and use a monitor LLM to reason over these rules based on the attributed text, making the detection decisions more explainable. We conduct a comprehensive evaluation on tool-use agent benchmarks and long-context understanding datasets. The experimental results demonstrate that AgentWatcher can effectively detect prompt injection and maintain utility without attacks."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fdc38bb6e81322b17b9f62890cb85c324464c461.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Interpretability and Analysis of Models for NLP"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission"}},"tmdate":1792185004456,"tcdate":1791744270280,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission4429/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission4429/Authors"],"forum":"NNbyBLNGSa","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4429,"cdate":1791744270280,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Responsible_NLP_Checklist_Status","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1792185004456,"odate":1791894541772,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","id":"NNbyBLNGSa","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.01194v2"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|agentwatcher_a_rulebased_prompt_injection_monitor"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.01194"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2604-01194,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yanting Wang and Wei Zou and Runpeng Geng and Jinyuan Jia},\n  title={AgentWatcher: A Rule-based Prompt Injection Monitor},\n  year={2026},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1775001600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2604.01194},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.01194}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) and their applications, such as agents, are highly vulnerable to prompt injection attacks. State-of-the-art prompt injection detection methods have the following limitations: (1) their effectiveness degrades significantly as context length increases, and (2) they lack explicit rules that define what constitutes prompt injection, causing detection decisions to be implicit, opaque, and difficult to reason about. In this work, we propose AgentWatcher to address the above two limitations. To address the first limitation, AgentWatcher attributes the LLM's output (e.g., the action of an agent) to a small set of causally influential context segments. By focusing detection on a relatively short text, AgentWatcher can be scalable to long contexts. To address the second limitation, we define a set of rules specifying what does and does not constitute a prompt injection, and use a monitor LLM to reason over these rules based on the attributed text, making the detection decisions more explainable. We conduct a comprehensive evaluation on tool-use agent benchmarks and long-context understanding datasets. The experimental results demonstrate that AgentWatcher can effectively detect prompt injection and maintain utility without attacks. The code is available at https://github.com/wang-yanting/AgentWatcher."},"title":{"value":"AgentWatcher: A Rule-based Prompt Injection Monitor"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yanting Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Wei Zou","username":"~Wei_Zou4"},{"fullname":"Runpeng Geng","username":""},{"fullname":"Jinyuan Jia","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791956354305,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2604-01194"],"tcdate":1791956351301,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Wei_Zou4"],"forum":"o3kRr4GURr","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":181947,"cdate":1775001600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791956354305,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"o3kRr4GURr","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Lightweight monitors detect goal drift in a reasoning model’s hidden states and trigger textual self-interventions to improve prompt-injection robustness"},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["prompt injection","language model security","reasoning intervention","adaptive robustness","goal drift"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection remains an unresolved security challenge for large reasoning models (LRMs) that interact with external content. Existing defenses perform well on static benchmarks yet remain vulnerable to stronger, optimization-based adaptive attacks. We introduce self-intervention, a plug-in defense strategy based on iterative textual interventions. An add-on monitor head detects goal drift from the model’s hidden states during generation and triggers reminder text in its ongoing reasoning. It requires neither an additional language model to guide interventions nor updates to the underlying reasoning model’s weights. Experiments show that self-intervention features improved robustness against strong adaptive attacks, including only 1% attack success rate against a state-of-the-art RL-attack, while preserving benign utility."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026reasoning,\ntitle={Reasoning Self-Intervention for Prompt Injection Robustness},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=gy8Tu1Bq4l},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Reasoning Self-Intervention for Prompt Injection Robustness"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1e67077505ccffc716bae8d483541fef8e910dbd.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791234348309,"tcdate":1789772949757,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission50086/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission50086/Authors"],"forum":"gy8Tu1Bq4l","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":50086,"cdate":1789772949757,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission50086/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791234348309,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"gy8Tu1Bq4l","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.00750v2"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"rahman|bypassing_prompt_injection_detectors_through_evasive_injections"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.00750"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2602-00750,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Md Jahedur Rahman and Ihsen Alouani},\n  title={Bypassing Prompt Injection Detectors through Evasive Injections},\n  year={2026},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1769904000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2602.00750},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.00750}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in interactive and retrieval-augmented systems, but they remain vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, where injected secondary prompts force the model to deviate from the user's instructions to execute a potentially malicious task defined by the adversary. Recent work shows that ML models trained on activation shifts from LLMs' hidden layers can detect such drift. In this paper, we demonstrate that these detectors are not robust to adaptive adversaries. We propose a multi-probe evasion attack that appends an adversarially optimised suffix to poisoned inputs, jointly optimising a universal suffix to simultaneously fool all layer-wise drift detectors while preserving the effectiveness of the underlying injection. Using a modified Greedy Coordinate Gradient (GCG) approach, we generate universal suffixes that make prompt injections consistently evasive across multiple probes simultaneously. On Phi-3 3.8B and Llama-3 8B, a single suffix achieves attack success rates of 93.91% and 99.63% in successfully evading all detectors simultaneously. These results show that activation-based task drift detectors are highly vulnerable to adaptive prompt injection attacks, motivating stronger defences against such threats. We also propose a defence based on adversarial suffix augmentation: we generate multiple suffixes, append one at random during forward passes, and train detectors on the resulting activations. This approach is found to be effective against evasive attacks."},"title":{"value":"Bypassing Prompt Injection Detectors through Evasive Injections"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Md Jahedur Rahman","username":""},{"fullname":"Ihsen Alouani","username":"~Ihsen_Alouani1"}]}},"tmdate":1785855753535,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2602-00750"],"tcdate":1785855747747,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Ihsen_Alouani1"],"forum":"ODP8NkQ5xM","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":116688,"cdate":1769904000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1785855753535,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"ODP8NkQ5xM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.15219v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"shi|promptarmor_simple_yet_effective_prompt_injection_defenses"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tianneng_Shi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaijie_Zhu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhun_Wang:","~Yuqi_Jia1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Will_Cai:","~Weida_Liang1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haonan_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hend_Alzahrani:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Joshua_Lu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kenji_Kawaguchi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Basel_Alomair:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xuandong_Zhao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:William_Yang_Wang:","~Neil_Gong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Wenbo_Guo_0002:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dawn_Song:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.15219"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2507-15219,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Tianneng Shi and Kaijie Zhu and Zhun Wang and Yuqi Jia and Will Cai and Weida Liang and Haonan Wang and Hend Alzahrani and Joshua Lu and Kenji Kawaguchi and Basel Alomair and Xuandong Zhao and William Yang Wang and Neil Gong and Wenbo Guo and Dawn Song},\n  title={PromptArmor: Simple yet Effective Prompt Injection Defenses},\n  year={2025},\n  month={July},\n  cdate={1751328000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2507.15219},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.15219}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Despite their potential, recent research has demonstrated that LLM agents are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, where malicious prompts are injected into the agent's input, causing it to perform an attacker-specified task rather than the intended task provided by the user. In this paper, we present PromptArmor, a simple yet effective defense against prompt injection attacks. Specifically, PromptArmor prompts an off-the-shelf LLM to detect and remove potential injected prompts from the input before the agent processes it. Our results show that PromptArmor can accurately identify and remove injected prompts. For example, using GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, or o4-mini, PromptArmor achieves both a false positive rate and a false negative rate below 1% on the AgentDojo benchmark. Moreover, after removing injected prompts with PromptArmor, the attack success rate drops to below 1%. We also demonstrate PromptArmor's effectiveness against adaptive attacks and explore different strategies for prompting an LLM. We recommend that PromptArmor be adopted as a standard baseline for evaluating new defenses against prompt injection attacks."},"title":{"value":"PromptArmor: Simple yet Effective Prompt Injection Defenses"},"authors":{"value":["Tianneng Shi","Kaijie Zhu","Zhun Wang","Yuqi Jia","Will Cai","Weida Liang","Haonan Wang","Hend Alzahrani","Joshua Lu","Kenji Kawaguchi","Basel Alomair","Xuandong Zhao","William Yang Wang","Neil Gong","Wenbo Guo","Dawn Song"]}},"tmdate":1785854938162,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2507-15219"],"tcdate":1758787728230,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tianneng_Shi1"],"forum":"zPc2RUUL0m","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":628351,"cdate":1751328000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785854938162,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"zPc2RUUL0m","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.104.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|webinject_prompt_injection_attack_to_web_agents"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.104"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/WangBSHZG25,\n  author={Xilong Wang and John Bloch and Zedian Shao and Yuepeng Hu and Shuyan Zhou and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={WebInject: Prompt Injection Attack to Web Agents},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={2010-2030},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.104},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multi-modal large language model (MLLM)-based web agents interact with webpage environments by generating actions based on screenshots of the webpages. In this work, we propose WebInject, a prompt injection attack that manipulates the webpage environment to induce a web agent to perform an attacker-specified action. Our attack adds a perturbation to the raw pixel values of the rendered webpage. After these perturbed pixels are mapped into a screenshot, the perturbation induces the web agent to perform the attacker-specified action. We formulate the task of finding the perturbation as an optimization problem. A key challenge in solving this problem is that the mapping between raw pixel values and screenshot is non-differentiable, making it difficult to backpropagate gradients to the perturbation. To overcome this, we train a neural network to approximate the mapping and apply projected gradient descent to solve the reformulated optimization problem. Extensive evaluation on multiple datasets shows that WebInject is highly effective and significantly outperforms baselines."},"title":{"value":"WebInject: Prompt Injection Attack to Web Agents"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xilong Wang","username":"~Xilong_Wang1"},{"fullname":"John Bloch","username":""},{"fullname":"Zedian Shao","username":""},{"fullname":"Yuepeng Hu","username":""},{"fullname":"Shuyan Zhou","username":""},{"fullname":"Neil Zhenqiang Gong","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784715575724,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/WangBSHZG25"],"tcdate":1784715572343,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xilong_Wang1"],"forum":"CNzRfJNtQ5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":90749,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784715575724,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"CNzRfJNtQ5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.00843v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"pan|oet_optimizationbased_prompt_injection_evaluation_toolkit"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinsheng_Pan:","~Xiaogeng_Liu1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chaowei_Xiao:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.00843"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-00843,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jinsheng Pan and Xiaogeng Liu and Chaowei Xiao},\n  title={OET: Optimization-based prompt injection Evaluation Toolkit},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.00843},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.00843}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in natural language understanding and generation, enabling their widespread adoption across various domains. However, their susceptibility to prompt injection attacks poses significant security risks, as adversarial inputs can manipulate model behavior and override intended instructions. Despite numerous defense strategies, a standardized framework to rigorously evaluate their effectiveness, especially under adaptive adversarial scenarios, is lacking. To address this gap, we introduce OET, an optimization-based evaluation toolkit that systematically benchmarks prompt injection attacks and defenses across diverse datasets using an adaptive testing framework. Our toolkit features a modular workflow that facilitates adversarial string generation, dynamic attack execution, and comprehensive result analysis, offering a unified platform for assessing adversarial robustness. Crucially, the adaptive testing framework leverages optimization methods with both white-box and black-box access to generate worst-case adversarial examples, thereby enabling strict red-teaming evaluations. Extensive experiments underscore the limitations of current defense mechanisms, with some models remaining susceptible even after implementing security enhancements."},"title":{"value":"OET: Optimization-based prompt injection Evaluation Toolkit"},"authors":{"value":["Jinsheng Pan","Xiaogeng Liu","Chaowei Xiao"]}},"tmdate":1767879654100,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-00843"],"tcdate":1767879579966,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xiaogeng_Liu1"],"forum":"H8f7AQYDn4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":735674,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1767879654100,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"H8f7AQYDn4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CODASPY 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CODASPY/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"jacob|promptshield_deployable_detection_for_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Dennis_Jacob1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hend_Alzahrani:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhanhao_Hu:","~Basel_Alomair1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:David_A._Wagner_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3714393.3726501"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/codaspy/JacobAHA025,\n  author={Dennis Jacob and Hend Alzahrani and Zhanhao Hu and Basel Alomair and David A. Wagner},\n  title={PromptShield: Deployable Detection for Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={341-352},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3714393.3726501},\n  booktitle={CODASPY},\n  crossref={conf/codaspy/2025}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"PromptShield: Deployable Detection for Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Dennis Jacob","Hend Alzahrani","Zhanhao Hu","Basel Alomair","David A. Wagner"]}},"tmdate":1753812874447,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1750606957950,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dennis_Jacob1"],"forum":"ShhpI7EO3N","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":564082,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753812874447,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ShhpI7EO3N","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.15145v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"jacob|promptshield_deployable_detection_for_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hend_Alzahrani:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zhanhao_Hu:","~Basel_Alomair1","~David_A._Wagner2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.15145"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2501-15145,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Dennis Jacob and Hend Alzahrani and Zhanhao Hu and Basel Alomair and David Wagner},\n  title={PromptShield: Deployable Detection for Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2025},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2501.15145},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2501.15145}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Current application designers have moved to integrate large language models (LLMs) into their products. These LLM-integrated applications are vulnerable to prompt injection vulnerabilities. While attempts have been made to address this problem by building a detector that can monitor inputs to the LLM and detect attacks, we find that many detectors are not yet suitable for practical deployment. To support research in this area, we design the PromptShield benchmark for evaluating practical prompt injection detectors. We also construct a new detector, the PromptShield detector, which achieves significantly better performance at detecting prompt injection attacks than any prior scheme. Our work suggests that larger models, more training data, appropriate metrics, and careful curation of training data can contribute to strong detector performance."},"title":{"value":"PromptShield: Deployable Detection for Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Dennis Jacob","Hend Alzahrani","Zhanhao Hu","Basel Alomair","David Wagner"]}},"tmdate":1753812874213,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1740770295471,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dennis_Jacob1"],"forum":"EYxNlR3us9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":342395,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753812874213,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"EYxNlR3us9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models","Prompt Injection","Multi-Agent Systems","LLM Security","LLM Safety"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/da813397d74e6a6a9de63ef9d516aea0cffb8a2f.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) grow increasingly powerful, multi-agent systems—where multiple LLMs collaborate to tackle complex tasks—are becoming more prevalent in modern AI applications. Most safety research, however, has focused on vulnerabilities in single-agent LLMs. These include prompt injection attacks, where malicious prompts embedded in external content trick the LLM into executing unintended or harmful actions, compromising the victim’s application. In this paper, we reveal a more dangerous vector: LLM-to-LLM prompt injection within multi-agent systems. We introduce Prompt Infection, a novel attack where malicious prompts self-replicate across interconnected agents, behaving much like a computer virus. This attack poses severe threats, including data theft, scams, misinformation, and system-wide disruption, all while propagating silently through the system. Our extensive experiments demonstrate that multi-agent systems are highly susceptible, even when agents do not directly share communications. To address this, we propose LLM Tagging, a defense mechanism that, when combined with existing safeguards, significantly mitigates infection spread. This work underscores the urgent need for advanced security measures as multi-agent LLM systems become more widely adopted."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nlee2025prompt,\ntitle={Prompt Infection: {LLM}-to-{LLM} Prompt Injection within Multi-Agent Systems},\nauthor={Donghyun Lee and Mo Tiwari},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=NAbqM2cMjD}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Prompt Infection: LLM-to-LLM Prompt Injection within Multi-Agent Systems"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/621c3f6a6d0a6aef5c7b3d2a38d00d4ffb8badb2.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"lee|prompt_infection_llmtollm_prompt_injection_within_multiagent_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["~Donghyun_Lee2","~Mo_Tiwari1"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Donghyun Lee","Mo Tiwari"]}},"tmdate":1738735821624,"tcdate":1727449548360,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission10776/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission10776/Authors"],"forum":"NAbqM2cMjD","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":10776,"cdate":1727449548360,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission10776/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738735821624,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"NAbqM2cMjD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 October Submission"},"research_area_keywords":{"value":"prompting, scaling, security"},"keywords":{"value":["prompt injection","defense"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026secinfer,\ntitle={SecInfer: Preventing Prompt Injection via Inference-time Scaling},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - October 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=glCWItRP3r},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"SecInfer: Preventing Prompt Injection via Inference-time Scaling"},"languages_studied":{"value":["eng"]},"contribution_types":{"value":"NLP engineering experiment"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection attacks pose a pervasive threat to the security of Large Language Models (LLMs). State-of-the-art prevention-based defenses typically rely on fine-tuning an LLM to enhance its security, but they achieve limited effectiveness against strong attacks. In this work, we propose \\emph{SecInfer}, a novel defense against prompt injection attacks built on \\emph{inference-time scaling}, an emerging paradigm that boosts LLM capability by allocating more compute resources for reasoning during inference. SecInfer consists of two key steps: \\emph{system-prompt-guided sampling}, which generates multiple responses for a given input by exploring diverse reasoning paths through a varied set of system prompts, and \\emph{target-task-guided aggregation}, which selects the response most likely to accomplish the intended task. Extensive experiments show that, by leveraging additional compute at inference, SecInfer effectively mitigates both existing and adaptive prompt injection attacks, outperforming state-of-the-art defenses as well as existing inference-time scaling approaches."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7214b4c7f743c53292ffadefb031a69cbec1dc14.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Language Modeling"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission"}},"tmdate":1792184995571,"tcdate":1791597261603,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission2697/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/Submission2697/Authors"],"forum":"glCWItRP3r","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2697,"cdate":1791597261603,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Responsible_NLP_Checklist_Status","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1792184995571,"odate":1791894541772,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/October","id":"glCWItRP3r","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Token-level KL divergence can misrank prompt-injection robustness in diffusion language models because predictive sensitivity does not identify which output changes determine the downstream action."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Desk Rejected Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["diffusion language models","prompt injection","agent security","KL divergence","robustness evaluation","decision preservation"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Masked diffusion language models expose a predictive distribution at every output position, which makes the Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence between a clean run and a prompt-injected run a convenient score for comparing decoders. We ask whether this score can rank decoders by their robustness to prompt injection. We study Dream-7B and LLaDA-8B on templated record-lookup cases and on 48 single-action tool cases in AgentDojo, averaging the KL of each output position at the step where that position is fixed. On the 32 primary lookup cases, Dream with a raised end-of-sequence (EOS) logit has mean KL 0.080 nats and follows the injection in 9 cases, whereas Dream with a penalty on the attacker’s target tokens, an oracle that assumes the target is known, has 1.101 nats and follows it in 0. Most of this KL comes from answers that keep the correct label but drop the sentence around it. A control that varies label length and requested answer format independently orders these two decoders correctly, but across its four Dream decoders every decision-blind score we tested has rank correlation −0.40 with attack counts, including the mean after subtracting the KL caused by a matched benign note, which repairs the focal comparison in both panels. Changed tokens still carry almost all of the KL; which of them matter depends on the decision rule. We show that per-position divergences cannot identify decision preservation without the decision rule, and give a margin condition under which stable decision-relevant tokens preserve an authorized action. A KL score says something about safety only once we know which output tokens decide the action."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2026when,\ntitle={{WHEN} {TOKEN}-{LEVEL} {DIVERGENCE} {MISRANKS} {PROMPT}-{INJECTION} {ROBUSTNESS}},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=HNkOvbXTb2}\n}"},"title":{"value":"WHEN TOKEN-LEVEL DIVERGENCE MISRANKS PROMPT-INJECTION ROBUSTNESS"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/38464296fcf4513e095cc0180aefe53b559cbf5f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Desk_Rejected_Submission"}},"tmdate":1792000082697,"tcdate":1789800621561,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55994/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55994/Authors"],"forum":"HNkOvbXTb2","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":55994,"cdate":1789800621561,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission55994/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Desk_Rejected_Submission"],"mdate":1792000082697,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"HNkOvbXTb2","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.07104v3"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|extended_to_reality_prompt_injection_in_3d_environments"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07104"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2602-07104,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Zhuoheng Li and Ying Chen},\n  title={Extended to Reality: Prompt Injection in 3D Environments},\n  year={2026},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1769904000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2602.07104},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07104}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have advanced the capabilities to interpret and act on visual input in 3D environments, empowering diverse applications such as robotics and situated conversational agents. When MLLMs reason over camera-captured views of the physical world, a new attack surface emerges: an attacker can place text-bearing physical objects in the environment to override MLLMs' intended task. While prior work has studied prompt injection in the text domain and through digitally edited 2D images, limited attention has been paid to how these attacks function in 3D environments. To bridge the gap, we introduce PI3D, a prompt injection attack against MLLMs in 3D environments, realized through text-bearing object placement rather than digital image edits. We formulate and solve the problem of identifying an effective pose (position and orientation) for a 3D object with injected text, where the attacker's goal is to induce the MLLM to perform the injected task while ensuring that the object placement remains physically plausible. Experiment results demonstrate that PI3D is an effective attack against multiple MLLMs under diverse camera trajectories. We further evaluate a range of defenses and show that they are not sufficient to reliably defend against PI3D."},"title":{"value":"Extended to Reality: Prompt Injection in 3D Environments"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Zhuoheng Li","username":"~Zhuoheng_Li2"},{"fullname":"Ying Chen","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791851629895,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2602-07104"],"tcdate":1791851624439,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Zhuoheng_Li2"],"forum":"g4zLM9iH0u","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":174455,"cdate":1769904000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791851629895,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"g4zLM9iH0u","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"DualView prevents immediate and stored indirect prompt injection in computer agents by keeping untrusted data symbolized in the agent's view while preserving original data for humans and non-agent programs."},"keywords":{"value":["AI agent security","indirect prompt injection","Dual LLM pattern"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Personal AI agents that run on the user's local machine, such as OpenClaw, automate tasks by accessing the network, file system, and shell. This access exposes them to indirect prompt injection (IPI) attacks. Prior Dual LLM defenses prevent attacker instructions from steering tool-call decisions by replacing untrusted data with symbols that the agent can reference but not read. However, they track untrusted data only inside the agent's context, so an attacker's prompt can return as trusted data after the agent saves and rereads it, causing stored IPI. We present DualView, which extends untrusted data tracking from the agent's context to the local files while preserving original data for humans and non-agent programs. In AgentView, the agent sees untrusted data as symbols even after writing and rereading it, which blocks stored IPI. HumanView exposes original data to humans and non-agent programs. DualView routes tool calls and synchronizes data across the two views. DualView deploys as a plugin using only tool hooks, without changing the agent's tool-call logic or tool implementations. DualView deterministically prevents instructions in untrusted data from directly steering the agent's tool calls, and this guarantee is not limited to the evaluated attack templates. In our evaluation on an IPI benchmark and PinchBench, DualView blocked every tested instruction-injection IPI attack, including stored IPI, while utility on PinchBench dropped by 1.8 and 6.4 percentage points from the unprotected baseline on Claude Haiku 4.5 and Claude Sonnet 4.6, respectively."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026dualview,\ntitle={DualView: Preventing Indirect Prompt Injection in Computer Agents},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=eWWm1KDHBz},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"DualView: Preventing Indirect Prompt Injection in Computer Agents"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8cad748c2b0a7e76de773c70186ecdd840cb52fa.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791233675021,"tcdate":1789746774406,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission45371/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission45371/Authors"],"forum":"eWWm1KDHBz","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":45371,"cdate":1789746774406,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission45371/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791233675021,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"eWWm1KDHBz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Prompt Injection","Detection Defense","LLM Agent Security"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection attacks insert malicious instructions into an LLM's input to steer it toward an attacker-chosen task instead of the intended one. Existing detection defenses typically classify any input containing an instruction as malicious, leading to misclassification of benign inputs containing instructions that align with the intended task. In this work, we account for the instruction hierarchy and distinguish among three categories: inputs with misaligned instructions, inputs with aligned instructions, and non-instruction inputs. We introduce AlignSentinel, an alignment-aware detection method that flags an input only when the instruction it carries overrides the higher-priority instruction rather than whenever it carries an instruction at all, using a three-class classifier over features derived from the LLM's attention maps. To support evaluation, we construct the first systematic benchmark containing inputs from all three categories. Experiments on both our benchmark and existing ones—where inputs with aligned instructions are largely absent—show that AlignSentinel accurately detects inputs with misaligned instructions and substantially outperforms baselines."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026alignsentinel,\ntitle={AlignSentinel: Alignment-Aware Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=3c9mBsDi83},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"AlignSentinel: Alignment-Aware Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7ecf8bf3341942b69b5282f2df65674bc9330d7d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791231812835,"tcdate":1789676676094,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34367/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34367/Authors"],"forum":"3c9mBsDi83","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":34367,"cdate":1789676676094,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission34367/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791231812835,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"3c9mBsDi83","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"TLDR":{"value":"Contextual Prompt Injection (CPI) shows that attacker-controlled repository context can mislead LLM-based MCP scanners: false remediation claims suppress genuine findings in over half of baseline-positive servers without changing vulnerable code."},"keywords":{"value":["Contextual Prompt Injection","Model Context Protocol (MCP)","LLM-based Security Scanners","LLM Security","Vulnerability Detection"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language model (LLM)-based Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server scanners increasingly analyze not only executable code but also repository-level context, such as README files, tool descriptions, and code comments. Because this context can be freely manipulated by MCP maintainers, it creates an overlooked attack surface where a malicious maintainer may influence scanner judgments through security claims. Building on this insight, we introduce Contextual Prompt Injection (CPI), a black-box attack that uses a scanner’s own findings to construct vulnerability-specific remediation narratives, claiming that reported issues have been fixed while leaving the relevant executable implementation unchanged. To enable realistic evaluation, we curate a dataset containing 372 real-world MCP Servers, including 45 with verified vulnerabilities. We evaluate CPI against three LLM-based MCP security scanners and existing benchmarks. Evaluation with manual verification shows that CPI suppresses at least one genuine vulnerability finding in more than half of baseline-positive repositories for each evaluated scanner. Such findings demonstrate that LLM-based scanners can prioritize attacker-controlled remediation claims over contradictory code evidence, highlighting the need to treat repository context as untrusted input and independently verify claimed fixes."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026cpi,\ntitle={{CPI}: Contextual Prompt Injection against {MCP} Server Scanners},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=7TImfQIpSU},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CPI: Contextual Prompt Injection against MCP Server Scanners"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/d20e599286ba11516b9067038e413243419e49bb.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791230267007,"tcdate":1789548477139,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission24295/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission24295/Authors"],"forum":"7TImfQIpSU","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":24295,"cdate":1789548477139,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission24295/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791230267007,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"7TImfQIpSU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Prompt injection detection; activation-based monitoring; LLM agent safety; interface shift; distribution shift; threshold calibration; probe transferability."]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/42efa830ec6ae8e33b52defdd8b586eb2f60dcef.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"A prompt-injection probe reads a language model's hidden states and flags a retrieved document that carries an instruction the user did not give. When an\napplication changes the interface, the way the task and the document are laid out in\nthe prompt, such a probe can break. The usual fix is a new threshold or a new linear\nreadout, judged by passing the test on attacks against clean documents again. In this\npaper, we first show that changing only the interface breaks two released TaskTracker\nprobes in 11 of 12 cells, and that a new readout passes that test again in all 11. Then,\nwe show that passing again can hide two failures that also occur without the shift: a threshold that fires on harmless\nedits of the document, and a score that misses attacks from another source. We find\nthat adding a kind of text to the repair's data removes its failure on held-out text of\nthat kind: a refit fitted on two attack sources, with harmless how-to steps in both its labels and its threshold, set per kind of text, passes all these tests in 78 of 80 random splits of its data (20 per shifted probe-interface pair). But on a new attack source it fails again: it misses at least 0.57 of its attacks, while the same refit without how-to steps catches them but fires on most how-to steps and quoted injections. So a repaired probe should be tested on harmless text and\nattacks that were not in its data."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026is,\ntitle={Is a Repaired Prompt-Injection Probe Really Repaired?},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=TiwD9K82Qx},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Is a Repaired Prompt-Injection Probe Really Repaired?"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/dc613f68792b1cd9a08b6478d829c120d890b850.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791226863049,"tcdate":1789198559537,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission14929/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission14929/Authors"],"forum":"TiwD9K82Qx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":14929,"cdate":1789198559537,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission14929/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing"],"mdate":1791226863049,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"TiwD9K82Qx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Visual Prompt Injection","Mobile Agents"]},"primary_area":{"value":"applications to computer vision, audio, language, and other modalities"},"abstract":{"value":"Mobile agents increasingly rely on screenshots as their only view of an application. This creates a basic security problem because genuine controls and attacker written instructions occupy the same visual space. An instruction placed in a message, review, or pop up can therefore redirect the next action without changing the agent or the application. Existing defenses usually either convert the screen into text, which discards layout, or alter the victim model, which limits their use with closed systems. We introduce \\ScreenLocate, a defense that uses the trusted task to find visual regions likely to redirect the agent and removes only those regions before inference. \\Lite provides a lexical version of this idea, while \\Ours also uses screen geometry, repairs directives split across adjacent text regions, and fills selected areas from their local boundaries. On the scenario disjoint \\AgentHazard test, \\Ours lowers attack success from $45.4$--$52.0\\%$ to $1.3$--$3.5\\%$ across three multimodal agents with a change of at most $2.62$ points in benign task performance. Under nine semantic presentation mutations, the fixed lexical variant lowers attack success by $31.4$--$42.4$ points across two primary victims and an additional Qwen3-VL-8B victim. Our experiments distinguish blocking the attacker from recovering the intended task, which reveals when a seemingly successful defense merely causes an invalid action. The results support a simple principle. Visual prompt injection should be handled as a localized intervention whose success is evaluated through the action taken by the agent."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026screenlocate,\ntitle={ScreenLocate: Defending Mobile Agents Against Visual Prompt Injection},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=6El44NUEDo},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"ScreenLocate: Defending Mobile Agents Against Visual Prompt Injection"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3b8c241c7643e74c300cc3234435b785d730eda4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791221435562,"tcdate":1788159145543,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission6713/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission6713/Authors"],"forum":"6El44NUEDo","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6713,"cdate":1788159145543,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission6713/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791221435562,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"6El44NUEDo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Zenodo"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"oktenli|authority_after_prompt_injection_policy_corpus_public_freeze"},"abstract":{"value":"Pre-provider, pre-confirmatory immutable timestamp package for the author-approved Draft 2 AgentDojo policy corpus and the 42/97-task primary support mask of the study \"Authority After Prompt Injection: An Empirical Study of Containment, Restoration Seeking, and Self-Report Fidelity in Tool-Using LLM Agents\". The package also contains the predeclared benchmark-reference conformance protocol (v1.1) and the exact audit code, so both are public before the audit runs. The audit is read-only and cannot alter the frozen corpus or the primary support mask. No provider-backed pilot or confirmatory result is included. Task texts quoted from AgentDojo v1.2.2 remain under the MIT License; the notice is included as THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. Everything else is licensed under CC BY 4.0. This work was carried out independently by the author. It was not conducted within, funded by, or supervised by any program at Georgetown University, and the University bears no responsibility for its content."},"title":{"value":"Authority After Prompt Injection: Policy Corpus Public Freeze"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Burak Oktenli","username":"~Burak_Oktenli2"}]}},"tmdate":1791188327886,"pdate":1790726400000,"externalIds":["doi:10.5281/zenodo.23053856"],"tcdate":1791188327747,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~BURAK_OKTENLI1"],"forum":"pRU6ksWD54","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":114453,"cdate":1790751503590,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791188327886,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"pRU6ksWD54","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Security"},"keywords":{"value":["Security","LLM agents","explainability","prompt-injection defense","activation probing"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"Yes"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix C"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"B5_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix C"},"C3_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix B, Appendix C"},"A2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 8"},"B2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 8"},"B3_elaboration":{"value":"Section 8"},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1, Section 3.2"},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1"},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix B"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"B1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 3.1"},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"Section 8"},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026your,\ntitle={Your Agentic {LLM}s Secretly Encode {\\textquotedblleft}Prompt-Injection Awareness{\\textquotedblright}},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=A3g5uAWUYz},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Your Agentic LLMs Secretly Encode “Prompt-Injection Awareness”"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"Yes"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"N/A"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"Yes"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"Agentic LLMs are vulnerable to indirect prompt injection attacks, \\eg, malicious side-tasks injected into external tool results.\nWe ask: Do these models encode awareness-like latent signals that are predictive of IPI risk in multi-turn agentic settings?\nWe study this question from three aspects. \n(1) \\textbf{Probing}: Simple linear probes, trained on model hidden states, can reliably predict risk at detection points, achieving over 90\\% AUROC on unseen attacks, agent instructions, and task suites.\nThese results suggest that agentic LLMs indeed encode such awareness-like signals.\n(2) \\textbf{Explanation}: We introduce the bridge-explanation score, a new metric that measures how probe activations correlate with models' answers to diagnostic statements.\nWe discover distinct forms of latent IPI awareness: probes can align with either explicit prompt-injection presence or indirect operational cues.\n(3) \\textbf{Defense}: We implement an awareness-guided reasoning intervention, reducing attack success rate on difficult \\dataset{AgentDojo} suites from 47.9\\% to 1.8\\% and outperforming inference-time baselines.\nCode is available: \\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/Latent-IPI-awareness}."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/60e41aee9bc7112486b2757c0b770773d7816dee.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Language Modeling"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"no"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"Yes"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696904089,"tcdate":1779090973072,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission1514/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission1514/Authors"],"forum":"A3g5uAWUYz","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1514,"cdate":1779090973072,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission1514/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1790696904089,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"A3g5uAWUYz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|manipulating_multimodal_agents_via_crossmodal_prompt_injection"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3746027.3755211"},"abstract":{"value":"The emergence of multimodal large language models has redefined the agent paradigm by integrating language and vision modalities with external data sources, enabling agents to better interpret human instructions and execute increasingly complex tasks. However, in this paper, we identify a critical yet previously overlooked security vulnerability in multimodal agents: cross-modal prompt injection attacks. To exploit this vulnerability, we propose CrossInject, a novel attack framework in which attacker embeds adversarial perturbations across multiple modalities to align with target malicious content, allowing external instructions to hijack the agents' decision-making process and execute unauthorized tasks. Our approach incorporates two key coordinated components. First, we introduce Visual Latent Alignment, where we optimize adversarial features to the malicious instructions in the visual embedding space based on a text-to-image generative model, ensuring that adversarial images subtly encode cues for malicious task execution. Subsequently, we present Textual Guidance Enhancement, where a large language model is leveraged to construct the black-box defensive system prompt through adversarial meta-prompting and generate a malicious textual command based on it that steers the agents' output toward better compliance with attacker's requests. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method outperforms state-of-the-art attacks, achieving at least a +30.1% increase in attack success rates across diverse tasks. Furthermore, we validate our attack's effectiveness in real-world multimodal autonomous agents, highlighting its potential implications for safety-critical applications. Code can be found in https://github.com/Larry0454/CrossInject."},"title":{"value":"Manipulating Multimodal Agents via Cross-Modal Prompt Injection"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Le Wang"},{"fullname":"Zonghao Ying"},{"fullname":"Tianyuan Zhang"},{"fullname":"Siyuan Liang"},{"fullname":"Shengshan Hu"},{"fullname":"Mingchuan Zhang"},{"fullname":"Aishan Liu"},{"fullname":"Xianglong Liu"}]}},"tmdate":1789092113965,"pdate":1761523200000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1145/3746027.3755211"],"tcdate":1764317440928,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Le_Wang12"],"forum":"KD5Us5IYcK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":18923,"cdate":1761391368248,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Author_Removal","OpenReview.net/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789092113965,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"KD5Us5IYcK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP (Findings) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.1060.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"wen|defending_against_indirect_prompt_injection_by_instruction_detection"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1060"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/WenWYTXLDW25,\n  author={Tongyu Wen and Chenglong Wang and Xiyuan Yang and Haoyu Tang and Yueqi Xie and Lingjuan Lyu and Zhicheng Dou and Fangzhao Wu},\n  title={Defending against Indirect Prompt Injection by Instruction Detection},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={19472-19487},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1060},\n  booktitle={EMNLP (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2025f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) with external sources is becoming increasingly common, with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) being a prominent example. However, this integration introduces vulnerabilities of Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI) attacks, where hidden instructions embedded in external data can manipulate LLMs into executing unintended or harmful actions. We recognize that IPI attacks fundamentally rely on the presence of instructions embedded within external content, which can alter the behavioral states of LLMs. Can the effective detection of such state changes help us defend against IPI attacks? In this paper, we propose InstructDetector, a novel detection-based approach that leverages the behavioral states of LLMs to identify potential IPI attacks. Specifically, we demonstrate the hidden states and gradients from intermediate layers provide highly discriminative features for instruction detection. By effectively combining these features, InstructDetector achieves a detection accuracy of 99.60% in the in-domain setting and 96.90% in the out-of-domain setting, and reduces the attack success rate to just 0.03% on the BIPIA benchmark. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/MYVAE/Instruction-detection."},"title":{"value":"Defending against Indirect Prompt Injection by Instruction Detection"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Tongyu Wen","username":""},{"fullname":"Chenglong Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Xiyuan Yang","username":""},{"fullname":"Haoyu Tang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yueqi Xie","username":""},{"fullname":"Lingjuan Lyu","username":"~Lingjuan_Lyu1"},{"fullname":"Zhicheng Dou","username":""},{"fullname":"Fangzhao Wu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1787540811516,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/WenWYTXLDW25"],"tcdate":1787540801415,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Lingjuan_Lyu1"],"forum":"bb4mdiOPYL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":139199,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1787540811516,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"bb4mdiOPYL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.06311v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wen|defending_against_indirect_prompt_injection_by_instruction_detection"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tongyu_Wen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chenglong_Wang_0004:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiyuan_Yang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Haoyu_Tang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yueqi_Xie:","~Lingjuan_Lyu1","~Zhicheng_Dou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fangzhao_Wu:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.06311"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-06311,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Tongyu Wen and Chenglong Wang and Xiyuan Yang and Haoyu Tang and Yueqi Xie and Lingjuan Lyu and Zhicheng Dou and Fangzhao Wu},\n  title={Defending against Indirect Prompt Injection by Instruction Detection},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.06311},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.06311}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) with external sources is becoming increasingly common, with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) being a prominent example. However, this integration introduces vulnerabilities of Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI) attacks, where hidden instructions embedded in external data can manipulate LLMs into executing unintended or harmful actions. We recognize that IPI attacks fundamentally rely on the presence of instructions embedded within external content, which can alter the behavioral states of LLMs. Can the effective detection of such state changes help us defend against IPI attacks? In this paper, we propose InstructDetector, a novel detection-based approach that leverages the behavioral states of LLMs to identify potential IPI attacks. Specifically, we demonstrate the hidden states and gradients from intermediate layers provide highly discriminative features for instruction detection. By effectively combining these features, InstructDetector achieves a detection accuracy of 99.60% in the in-domain setting and 96.90% in the out-of-domain setting, and reduces the attack success rate to just 0.03% on the BIPIA benchmark. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/MYVAE/Instruction-detection."},"title":{"value":"Defending against Indirect Prompt Injection by Instruction Detection"},"authors":{"value":["Tongyu Wen","Chenglong Wang","Xiyuan Yang","Haoyu Tang","Yueqi Xie","Lingjuan Lyu","Zhicheng Dou","Fangzhao Wu"]}},"tmdate":1787540802924,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-06311"],"tcdate":1768185608045,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Tongyu_Wen1"],"forum":"nKHrmKT90r","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":743986,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1787540802924,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"nKHrmKT90r","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Prompt injection extended to 3D environments"},"venue":{"value":"COLM 2026"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the COLM Code of Ethics on https://colmweb.org/CoE.html"},"LLM_usage":{"value":"In accordance with the Policy on the use of Large Language Models in the Call for Papers https://colmweb.org/cfp.html, I certify that my submission discloses any substantive use of LLMs in the research and paper-writing process."},"keywords":{"value":["3D environments","prompt injection","multimodal large language models"]},"abstract":{"value":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have advanced the capabilities to interpret and act on visual input in 3D environments, empowering diverse applications such as robotics and situated conversational agents. When MLLMs reason over camera-captured views of the physical world, a new attack surface emerges: an attacker can place text-bearing physical objects in the environment to override MLLMs’ intended task. While prior work has studied prompt injection in the text domain and through digitally edited 2D images, limited attention has been paid to how these attacks function in 3D environments. To bridge the gap, we introduce PI3D, a prompt injection attack against MLLMs in 3D environments, realized through text-bearing object placement rather than digital image edits. We formulate and solve the problem of identifying an effective pose (position and orientation) for a 3D object with injected text, where the attacker’s goal is to induce the MLLM to perform the injected task while ensuring that the object placement remains physically plausible. Experiment results demonstrate that PI3D is an effective attack against multiple MLLMs under diverse camera trajectories. We further evaluate a range of defenses and show that they are not sufficient to reliably defend against PI3D."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nli2026extended,\ntitle={Extended to Reality: Prompt Injection in 3D Environments},\nauthor={Zhuoheng Li and Ying Chen},\nbooktitle={Third Conference on Language Modeling},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=tyHoomuiFy}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Extended to Reality: Prompt Injection in 3D Environments"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7ce50f5a028242b50b02fa652f53d4ba5503274b.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|extended_to_reality_prompt_injection_in_3d_environments"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhuoheng_Li2","~Ying_Chen38"]},"author_guide":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://colmweb.org/AuthorGuide.html"},"authors":{"value":["Zhuoheng Li","Ying Chen"]}},"tmdate":1787537161158,"pdate":1783543367494,"tcdate":1774365406043,"writers":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission694/Authors"],"signatures":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission694/Authors"],"forum":"tyHoomuiFy","license":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","number":694,"cdate":1774365406043,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission694/-/Full_Submission","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/-/Edit","colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference/Submission694/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1787537161158,"odate":1787537161070,"domain":"colmweb.org/COLM/2026/Conference","id":"tyHoomuiFy","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.13597v2"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"jia|alignsentinel_alignmentaware_detection_of_prompt_injection_attacks"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.13597"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2602-13597,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuqi Jia and Ruiqi Wang and Xilong Wang and Chong Xiang and Neil Gong},\n  title={AlignSentinel: Alignment-Aware Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2026},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1769904000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2602.13597},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.13597}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection attacks insert malicious instructions into an LLM's input to steer it toward an attacker-chosen task instead of the intended one. Existing detection defenses typically classify any input with instruction as malicious, leading to misclassification of benign inputs containing instructions that align with the intended task. In this work, we account for the instruction hierarchy and distinguish among three categories: inputs with misaligned instructions, inputs with aligned instructions, and non-instruction inputs. We introduce AlignSentinel, a three-class classifier that leverages features derived from LLM's attention maps to categorize inputs accordingly. To support evaluation, we construct the first systematic benchmark containing inputs from all three categories. Experiments on both our benchmark and existing ones--where inputs with aligned instructions are largely absent--show that AlignSentinel accurately detects inputs with misaligned instructions and substantially outperforms baselines."},"title":{"value":"AlignSentinel: Alignment-Aware Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuqi Jia","username":"~Yuqi_Jia1"},{"fullname":"Ruiqi Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Xilong Wang","username":"~Xilong_Wang1"},{"fullname":"Chong Xiang","username":""},{"fullname":"Neil Gong","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1785854938100,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2602-13597"],"tcdate":1784715572189,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xilong_Wang1"],"forum":"EPf4nHPdCl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":90746,"cdate":1769904000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1785854938100,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"EPf4nHPdCl","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.01354v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|wainjectbench_benchmarking_prompt_injection_detections_for_web_agents"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yinuo_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ruohan_Xu:","~Xilong_Wang1","~Yuqi_Jia1","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.01354"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2510-01354,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yinuo Liu and Ruohan Xu and Xilong Wang and Yuqi Jia and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={WAInjectBench: Benchmarking Prompt Injection Detections for Web Agents},\n  year={2025},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1759276800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2510.01354},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.01354}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Multiple prompt injection attacks have been proposed against web agents. At the same time, various methods have been developed to detect general prompt injection attacks, but none have been systematically evaluated for web agents. In this work, we bridge this gap by presenting the first comprehensive benchmark study on detecting prompt injection attacks targeting web agents. We begin by introducing a fine-grained categorization of such attacks based on the threat model. We then construct datasets containing both malicious and benign samples: malicious text segments generated by different attacks, benign text segments from four categories, malicious images produced by attacks, and benign images from two categories. Next, we systematize both text-based and image-based detection methods. Finally, we evaluate their performance across multiple scenarios. Our key findings show that while some detectors can identify attacks that rely on explicit textual instructions or visible image perturbations with moderate to high accuracy, they largely fail against attacks that omit explicit instructions or employ imperceptible perturbations. Our datasets and code are released at: https://github.com/Norrrrrrr-lyn/WAInjectBench."},"title":{"value":"WAInjectBench: Benchmarking Prompt Injection Detections for Web Agents"},"authors":{"value":["Yinuo Liu","Ruohan Xu","Xilong Wang","Yuqi Jia","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1785854938085,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2510-01354"],"tcdate":1764166584619,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"vPHu3YvsOG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706386,"cdate":1759276800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785854938085,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vPHu3YvsOG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.24967v4"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|secinfer_preventing_prompt_injection_via_inferencetime_scaling"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yupei_Liu:","~Yanting_Wang2","~Yuqi_Jia1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyuan_Jia_0001:","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.24967"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2509-24967,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yupei Liu and Yanting Wang and Yuqi Jia and Jinyuan Jia and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={SecInfer: Preventing Prompt Injection via Inference-time Scaling},\n  year={2025},\n  month={September},\n  cdate={1756684800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2509.24967},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.24967}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection attacks pose a pervasive threat to the security of Large Language Models (LLMs). State-of-the-art prevention-based defenses typically rely on fine-tuning an LLM to enhance its security, but they achieve limited effectiveness against strong attacks. In this work, we propose \\emph{SecInfer}, a novel defense against prompt injection attacks built on \\emph{inference-time scaling}, an emerging paradigm that boosts LLM capability by allocating more compute resources for reasoning during inference. SecInfer consists of two key steps: \\emph{system-prompt-guided sampling}, which generates multiple responses for a given input by exploring diverse reasoning paths through a varied set of system prompts, and \\emph{target-task-guided aggregation}, which selects the response most likely to accomplish the intended task. Extensive experiments show that, by leveraging additional compute at inference, SecInfer effectively mitigates both existing and adaptive prompt injection attacks, outperforming state-of-the-art defenses as well as existing inference-time scaling approaches."},"title":{"value":"SecInfer: Preventing Prompt Injection via Inference-time Scaling"},"authors":{"value":["Yupei Liu","Yanting Wang","Yuqi Jia","Jinyuan Jia","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1785854937905,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2509-24967"],"tcdate":1764166584312,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"mnO0ZicKag","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706393,"cdate":1756684800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785854937905,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"mnO0ZicKag","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACM Multimedia 2025"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MM/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|manipulating_multimodal_agents_via_crossmodal_prompt_injection"},"authorids":{"value":["~Le_Wang12","","~Tianyuan_Zhang3","","","","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3746027.3755211"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/mm/WangY00HZL025,\n  author={Le Wang and Zonghao Ying and Tianyuan Zhang and Siyuan Liang and Shengshan Hu and Mingchuan Zhang and Aishan Liu and Xianglong Liu},\n  title={Manipulating Multimodal Agents via Cross-Modal Prompt Injection},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={10955-10964},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3746027.3755211},\n  booktitle={ACM Multimedia},\n  crossref={conf/mm/2025}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Manipulating Multimodal Agents via Cross-Modal Prompt Injection"},"authors":{"value":["Le Wang","Zonghao Ying","Tianyuan Zhang","Siyuan Liang","Shengshan Hu","Mingchuan Zhang","Aishan Liu","Xianglong Liu"]}},"tmdate":1784016425956,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/mm/WangY00HZL025"],"tcdate":1774405611112,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Le_Wang12"],"forum":"enyw7Ht0oy","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":858747,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1784016425956,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"enyw7Ht0oy","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Backdoor Attacks, Prompt Injection Attacks, Large Language Models"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 December Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025backdoorpowered,\ntitle={Backdoor-Powered Prompt Injection Attacks Nullify Defense Methods},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - December 2024},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=mZBX2wFT1L},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Backdoor-Powered Prompt Injection Attacks Nullify Defense Methods"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment","Data resources"]},"abstract":{"value":"With the development of technology, large language models (LLMs) have dominated the downstream natural language processing (NLP) tasks. However, because of the LLMs' instruction-following abilities and inability to distinguish the instructions in the data content, such as web pages from search engines, the LLMs are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks. These attacks trick the LLMs into deviating from the original input instruction and executing the attackers' target instruction. \nRecently, various instruction hierarchy defense strategies are proposed to effectively defend against prompt injection attacks via fine-tuning.  \nIn this paper, we explore a more vicious attack that even nullify the instruction hierarchy: backdoor-powered prompt injection attacks, where the attackers utilize the backdoor attack for prompt injection attack purposes. Specifically, the attackers poison the supervised fine-tuning samples and insert the backdoor into the model.  Once the trigger is activated, the backdoored model executes the injected instruction surrounded by the trigger.  For evaluation we construct a benchmark and our experiments demonstrate that backdoor-powered prompt injection attacks are much more harmful than previous prompt injection attacks, nullifying the instruction hierarchy strategies."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/feb18c5a7a896b819fcc59c646ca87161de12752.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962105543,"tcdate":1734267635669,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/Submission843/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/Submission843/Authors"],"forum":"mZBX2wFT1L","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":843,"cdate":1734267635669,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/-/Ethics_Review_Flag","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962105543,"odate":1738780930893,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/December","id":"mZBX2wFT1L","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"safety and alignment, security and privacy"},"keywords":{"value":["safety","large language model","indirect prompt injection attack"]},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 May Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025defending,\ntitle={Defending against Indirect Prompt Injection by Instruction Detection},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jo6U8nxRip},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Defending against Indirect Prompt Injection by Instruction Detection"},"contribution_types":{"value":["Model analysis & interpretability"]},"abstract":{"value":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) with external  sources is becoming increasingly common, with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) being a prominent example. However, this integration introduces vulnerabilities of Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI) attacks, where hidden instructions embedded in external data can manipulate LLMs into executing unintended or harmful actions. We recognize that IPI attacks fundamentally rely on the presence of instructions embedded within external content, which can alter the behavioral states of LLMs. Can effectively detecting such state changes help us defend against IPI attacks? In this paper, we propose InstructDetector, a novel detection-based approach that leverages the behavioral states of LLMs to identify potential IPI attacks. Specifically, we demonstrate the hidden states and gradients from intermediate layers provide highly discriminative features for instruction detection. By effectively combining these features, InstructDetector achieves a detection accuracy of 99.60\\% in the in-domain setting and 96.90\\% in the out-of-domain setting, and reduces the attack success rate to just 0.03\\% on the BIPIA benchmark."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a529357b45245a0f7ac8441c4990c08d3e878b62.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Language Modeling"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962061824,"tcdate":1747577143114,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission1931/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission1931/Authors"],"forum":"jo6U8nxRip","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1931,"cdate":1747577143114,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission1931/-/Blind_Submission_License_Agreement","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962061824,"odate":1753766020949,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","id":"jo6U8nxRip","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICML 2026 Position Paper Track"},"TLDR":{"value":"Prompt Injection risks must be tackled interdisciplinarily."},"keywords":{"value":"Prompt Injections, Large Language Models, Risk Mitigation"},"primary_area":{"value":"risk_safety_policy"},"abstract":{"value":"As LLM-based systems become pervasive in consumer applications and high-stakes domains, prompt injections (PIs)---the insertion of malicious instructions to manipulate LLM outputs---present a persistent and growing threat. In this position paper, we argue that PIs constitute a socio-technical phenomenon that cannot be addressed by algorithmic defenses alone. Thus, navigating central challenges pertaining to trust and autonomy, responsibility and regulation, as well as innovation and utility requires an interdisciplinary approach integrating technical, legal, ethical, and economic expertise. We here introduce a three-dimensional classification schema to facilitate systematic risk assessment across disciplines, and demonstrate its utility with three case studies ranging from search manipulation to autonomous trading. We call on the research community to embrace interdisciplinary collaboration as an essential part of managing the risks PIs pose to individuals and society."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2026position,\ntitle={Position: Prompt Injection Risks Must Be Tackled Interdisciplinarily},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Lid78487qz}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Position: Prompt Injection Risks Must Be Tackled Interdisciplinarily"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e69dded752d2653ffbe56cf813d646d8199b872f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"janal|position_prompt_injection_risks_must_be_tackled_interdisciplinarily"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ruth_Janal1","~Lena_Kästner1","~Niklas_Kuehl1","~Ruben_Mayer1","~Sebastian_Roth1"]},"authors":{"value":["Ruth Janal","Lena Kästner","Niklas Kuehl","Ruben Mayer","Sebastian Roth"]}},"tmdate":1782341245725,"tcdate":1769185050998,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track","ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track/Submission607/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track/Submission607/Authors"],"forum":"Lid78487qz","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":607,"cdate":1769185050998,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track/Submission607/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track/-/Edit"],"mdate":1782341245725,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Position_Paper_Track","id":"Lid78487qz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP (Findings) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.242.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|backdoorpowered_prompt_injection_attacks_nullify_defense_methods"},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.242/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/ChenLSSH25,\n  author={Yulin Chen and Haoran Li and Yuan Sui and Yangqiu Song and Bryan Hooi},\n  title={Backdoor-Powered Prompt Injection Attacks Nullify Defense Methods},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={4508-4527},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.242/},\n  booktitle={EMNLP (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2025f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the development of technology, large language models (LLMs) have dominated the downstream natural language processing (NLP) tasks. However, because of the LLMs’ instruction-following abilities and inability to distinguish the instructions in the data content, such as web pages from search engines, the LLMs are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks. These attacks trick the LLMs into deviating from the original input instruction and executing the attackers’ target instruction. Recently, various instruction hierarchy defense strategies are proposed to effectively defend against prompt injection attacks via fine-tuning.In this paper, we explore more vicious attacks that nullify the prompt injection defense methods, even the instruction hierarchy: backdoor-powered prompt injection attacks, where the attackers utilize the backdoor attack for prompt injection attack purposes. Specifically, the attackers poison the supervised fine-tuning samples and insert the backdoor into the model. Once the trigger is activated, the backdoored model executes the injected instruction surrounded by the trigger. We construct a benchmark for comprehensive evaluation. Our experiments demonstrate that backdoor-powered prompt injection attacks are more harmful than previous prompt injection attacks, nullifying existing prompt injection defense methods, even the instruction hierarchy techniques."},"title":{"value":"Backdoor-Powered Prompt Injection Attacks Nullify Defense Methods"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yulin Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Haoran Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Yuan Sui","username":"~Yuan_Sui1"},{"fullname":"Yangqiu Song","username":""},{"fullname":"Bryan Hooi","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1780673620847,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/ChenLSSH25"],"tcdate":1780673611861,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yuan_Sui1"],"forum":"bUVINfmbun","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":42424,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1780673620847,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"bUVINfmbun","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.11513v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"salem|maatphor_automated_variant_analysis_for_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ahmed_Salem2","~Andrew_Paverd1","~Boris_Köpf1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.11513"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2312-11513,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ahmed Salem and Andrew Paverd and Boris Köpf},\n  title={Maatphor: Automated Variant Analysis for Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2023},\n  cdate={1672531200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2312.11513},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2312.11513}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection has emerged as a serious security threat to large language models (LLMs). At present, the current best-practice for defending against newly-discovered prompt injection techniques is to add additional guardrails to the system (e.g., by updating the system prompt or using classifiers on the input and/or output of the model.) However, in the same way that variants of a piece of malware are created to evade anti-virus software, variants of a prompt injection can be created to evade the LLM's guardrails. Ideally, when a new prompt injection technique is discovered, candidate defenses should be tested not only against the successful prompt injection, but also against possible variants. In this work, we present, a tool to assist defenders in performing automated variant analysis of known prompt injection attacks. This involves solving two main challenges: (1) automatically generating variants of a given prompt according, and (2) automatically determining whether a variant was effective based only on the output of the model. This tool can also assist in generating datasets for jailbreak and prompt injection attacks, thus overcoming the scarcity of data in this domain. We evaluate Maatphor on three different types of prompt injection tasks. Starting from an ineffective (0%) seed prompt, Maatphor consistently generates variants that are at least 60% effective within the first 40 iterations."},"title":{"value":"Maatphor: Automated Variant Analysis for Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Ahmed Salem","Andrew Paverd","Boris Köpf"]}},"tmdate":1779700552596,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1747741117680,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~ahmed_salem1"],"forum":"Zn5MQUVFvr","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":534649,"cdate":1672531200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779700552596,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Zn5MQUVFvr","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We presented RAPIDS, a cascade framework for detecting indirect prompt injection attacks in resumes at production scale."},"venue":{"value":"ACL 2026 Industry Track Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["Adversarial Attacks","Large Language Models","Prompt Injection","Resume Screening","Safety"]},"abstract":{"value":"The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into recruitment workflows has introduced a critical security vulnerability: indirect prompt injection attacks embedded within resumes can manipulate screening tools to override instructions, effectively jailbreaking the hiring process. Frontier LLMs can detect such anomalies, but deploying them at the scale required for high-volume recruitment is prohibitively slow and costly. At the same time, existing generic prompt injection detectors lack the domain specificity needed for nuanced resume attacks. To address this gap, we introduce RAPIDS, a scalable detection framework with three contributions. First, we release a synthetically generated dataset of injection snippets derived from curated attack seeds spanning multiple adversarial strategies to address data scarcity in this domain. Second, we fine-tune a lightweight Small Language Model (SLM) on this data that outperforms the best off-the-shelf detector by over 50% in relative F1 and approaches frontier LLM accuracy. Third, we propose a cascade architecture in which the fine-tuned SLM serves as a high-recall first stage followed by an LLM verifier. This design achieves ${\\geq}$ 98% end-to-end recall on both evaluated datasets while delivering a $21-24\\times$ latency reduction over standalone frontier LLMs (GPT-5-mini), bringing expected per-request latency to $115-171$ ms at roughly 3.5% of the API cost."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\naugey2026rapids,\ntitle={{RAPIDS}: Resume Attack Prompt Injection Detection at Scale},\nauthor={Yohann Augey and Joshua H. Levy and Arda Akdemir},\nbooktitle={The 64th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics -- Industry Track},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=68a4FXwzs7}\n}"},"title":{"value":"RAPIDS: Resume Attack Prompt Injection Detection at Scale"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f9f416d6949bd2f767da4b15fd96a60a4c64f3f8.pdf"},"submission_type":{"value":2},"copyright_form":{"value":"/attachment/c7c2eba3b6a7295eab7e1a380b0d35f3a7567332.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track"},"paperhash":{"value":"augey|rapids_resume_attack_prompt_injection_detection_at_scale"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yohann_Augey1","~Joshua_H._Levy2","~Arda_Akdemir2"]},"authors":{"value":["Yohann Augey","Joshua H. Levy","Arda Akdemir"]}},"tmdate":1776989862943,"pdate":1776491717858,"tcdate":1771127856340,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track","aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track/Submission440/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track/Submission440/Authors"],"forum":"68a4FXwzs7","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":440,"cdate":1771127856340,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track/Submission440/-/Revision"],"mdate":1776989862943,"odate":1776491717858,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/2026/Industry_Track","id":"68a4FXwzs7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM Security","Prompt Injection Attacks","Detection Defense"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection attacks insert malicious instructions into an LLM's input to steer it toward an attacker-chosen task instead of the intended one. Existing detection defenses typically classify any input with instruction as malicious, leading to misclassification of benign inputs containing instructions that align with the intended task. In this work, we account for the instruction hierarchy and distinguish among three categories: inputs with misaligned instructions, inputs with aligned instructions, and non-instruction inputs. We introduce AlignSentinel, a three-class classifier that leverages features derived from the LLM's attention maps to categorize inputs accordingly. To support evaluation, we construct the first systematic benchmark containing inputs from all three categories. Experiments on both our benchmark and existing ones--where inputs with aligned instructions are largely absent--show that AlignSentinel accurately detects inputs with misaligned instructions  and substantially outperforms baselines."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\njia2026alignsentinel,\ntitle={AlignSentinel: Alignment-Aware Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks},\nauthor={Yuqi Jia and Ruiqi Wang and Xilong Wang and Chong Xiang and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=yPgbdOdOPG}\n}"},"title":{"value":"AlignSentinel: Alignment-Aware Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2db699ac5e4a854bcbb0733ae243daa3b02821bc.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"jia|alignsentinel_alignmentaware_detection_of_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuqi_Jia1","~Ruiqi_Wang20","~Xilong_Wang1","~Chong_Xiang1","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"authors":{"value":["Yuqi Jia","Ruiqi Wang","Xilong Wang","Chong Xiang","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1770805092167,"tcdate":1758291248515,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission18833/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission18833/Authors"],"forum":"yPgbdOdOPG","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":18833,"cdate":1758291248515,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission18833/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission18833/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770805092167,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"yPgbdOdOPG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["Agent","Security","Prompt Injection"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"LLM agents are inherently vulnerable to *prompt injection attacks*. A simple and easy-to-deploy baseline defense is to directly prompt an *off-the-shelf* LLM to detect injected prompts; however, prior work has shown this approach to be largely ineffective. Importantly, these results were based on older LLMs with weaker reasoning capabilities. In this work, we revisit this idea in light of the strong reasoning capabilities of modern LLMs. The results show that, with a carefully designed system prompt, our PromptArmor can accurately *detect* and *remove* injected prompts by directly prompting a modern LLM. For example, PromptArmor using GPT-4o achieves both a *false positive rate* and a *false negative rate* below 1% on the AgentDojo benchmark, and below 5% on Open Prompt Injection and TensorTrust. We further evaluate PromptArmor against adaptive attacks and investigate alternative prompting strategies. Overall, our work shows that the previous conception of this approach as ineffective is no longer the case, and that prompting a strong, off-the-shelf LLM should now be regarded as a standard baseline for evaluating defenses against prompt injection."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nshi2026promptarmor,\ntitle={PromptArmor: An Essential Baseline for Prompt Injection Defenses},\nauthor={Tianneng Shi and Kaijie Zhu and Zhun Wang and Yuqi Jia and Will Cai and Weida Liang and Haonan Wang and Hend Alzahrani and Joshua Lu and Kenji Kawaguchi and Basel Alomair and Xuandong Zhao and William Yang Wang and Neil Zhenqiang Gong and Wenbo Guo and Dawn Song},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=IeNXtofK6T}\n}"},"title":{"value":"PromptArmor: An Essential Baseline for Prompt Injection Defenses"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/038476d7cdb2662c65fd36145bedadbe086abe4f.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"shi|promptarmor_an_essential_baseline_for_prompt_injection_defenses"},"authorids":{"value":["~Tianneng_Shi1","~Kaijie_Zhu1","~Zhun_Wang1","~Yuqi_Jia1","~Will_Cai1","~Weida_Liang1","~Haonan_Wang1","~Hend_Alzahrani1","~Joshua_Lu1","~Kenji_Kawaguchi1","~Basel_Alomair1","~Xuandong_Zhao1","~William_Yang_Wang2","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1","~Wenbo_Guo1","~Dawn_Song1"]},"authors":{"value":["Tianneng Shi","Kaijie Zhu","Zhun Wang","Yuqi Jia","Will Cai","Weida Liang","Haonan Wang","Hend Alzahrani","Joshua Lu","Kenji Kawaguchi","Basel Alomair","Xuandong Zhao","William Yang Wang","Neil Zhenqiang Gong","Wenbo Guo","Dawn Song"]}},"tmdate":1770804858904,"tcdate":1757812080568,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission4931/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission4931/Authors"],"forum":"IeNXtofK6T","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4931,"cdate":1757812080568,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission4931/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770804858904,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"IeNXtofK6T","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.03705v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|backdoorpowered_prompt_injection_attacks_nullify_defense_methods"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yulin_Chen:","~Haoran_Li3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yuan_Sui_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yangqiu_Song:","~Bryan_Hooi1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.03705"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2510-03705,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yulin Chen and Haoran Li and Yuan Sui and Yangqiu Song and Bryan Hooi},\n  title={Backdoor-Powered Prompt Injection Attacks Nullify Defense Methods},\n  year={2025},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1759276800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2510.03705},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.03705}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the development of technology, large language models (LLMs) have dominated the downstream natural language processing (NLP) tasks. However, because of the LLMs' instruction-following abilities and inability to distinguish the instructions in the data content, such as web pages from search engines, the LLMs are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks. These attacks trick the LLMs into deviating from the original input instruction and executing the attackers' target instruction. Recently, various instruction hierarchy defense strategies are proposed to effectively defend against prompt injection attacks via fine-tuning. In this paper, we explore more vicious attacks that nullify the prompt injection defense methods, even the instruction hierarchy: backdoor-powered prompt injection attacks, where the attackers utilize the backdoor attack for prompt injection attack purposes. Specifically, the attackers poison the supervised fine-tuning samples and insert the backdoor into the model. Once the trigger is activated, the backdoored model executes the injected instruction surrounded by the trigger. We construct a benchmark for comprehensive evaluation. Our experiments demonstrate that backdoor-powered prompt injection attacks are more harmful than previous prompt injection attacks, nullifying existing prompt injection defense methods, even the instruction hierarchy techniques."},"title":{"value":"Backdoor-Powered Prompt Injection Attacks Nullify Defense Methods"},"authors":{"value":["Yulin Chen","Haoran Li","Yuan Sui","Yangqiu Song","Bryan Hooi"]}},"tmdate":1769323279260,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2510-03705"],"tcdate":1768972960474,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Bryan_Hooi1"],"forum":"Rh03g6Ie59","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":778301,"cdate":1759276800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769323279260,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Rh03g6Ie59","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"USENIX Security Symposium 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity24-liu-yupei.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/USS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|formalizing_and_benchmarking_prompt_injection_attacks_and_defenses"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yupei_Liu:","~Yuqi_Jia1","~Runpeng_Geng1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyuan_Jia_0001:","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity24/presentation/liu-yupei"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/uss/LiuJGJG24,\n  author={Yupei Liu and Yuqi Jia and Runpeng Geng and Jinyuan Jia and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={Formalizing and Benchmarking Prompt Injection Attacks and Defenses},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  url={https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity24/presentation/liu-yupei},\n  booktitle={USENIX Security Symposium},\n  crossref={conf/uss/2024}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Formalizing and Benchmarking Prompt Injection Attacks and Defenses"},"authors":{"value":["Yupei Liu","Yuqi Jia","Runpeng Geng","Jinyuan Jia","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1767481582759,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727462534977,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuqi_Jia1"],"forum":"we4ksCWrcq","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":92717,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1767481582759,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"we4ksCWrcq","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2026 Conference Withdrawn Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["web agents","prompt injection attacks"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Multiple prompt injection attacks have been proposed against web agents. At the same time, various methods have been developed to detect general prompt injection attacks, but none have been systematically evaluated for web agents. In this work, we bridge this gap by presenting the first comprehensive benchmark study on detecting prompt injection attacks targeting web agents. We begin by introducing a fine-grained categorization of such attacks based on the threat model. We then construct datasets containing both malicious and benign samples: malicious text segments generated by different attacks, benign text segments from four categories, malicious images produced by attacks, and benign images from two categories. Next, we systematize both text-based and image-based detection methods. Finally, we evaluate their performance across multiple scenarios. Our key findings show that while some detectors can identify attacks that rely on explicit textual instructions or visible image perturbations with moderate to high accuracy, they largely fail against attacks that omit explicit instructions or employ imperceptible perturbations. Our datasets and code are released at: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/WAInjectBench-C51D."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nliu2025wainjectbench,\ntitle={{WAI}njectBench: Benchmarking Prompt Injection Detections for Web Agents},\nauthor={Yinuo Liu and Ruohan Xu and Xilong Wang and Yuqi Jia and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=bTYxaNh8R7}\n}"},"title":{"value":"WAInjectBench: Benchmarking Prompt Injection Detections for Web Agents"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/736ec053bfd103099e0a549de3931ba01859d3ba.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Withdrawn_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|wainjectbench_benchmarking_prompt_injection_detections_for_web_agents"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yinuo_Liu1","~Ruohan_Xu2","~Xilong_Wang1","~Yuqi_Jia1","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"authors":{"value":["Yinuo Liu","Ruohan Xu","Xilong Wang","Yuqi Jia","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1762962511059,"tcdate":1758137978658,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission9748/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission9748/Authors"],"forum":"bTYxaNh8R7","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":9748,"cdate":1758137978658,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission9748/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Withdrawn_Submission"],"mdate":1762962511059,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"bTYxaNh8R7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.14720v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hines|defending_against_indirect_prompt_injection_attacks_with_spotlighting"},"authorids":{"value":["~Keegan_Hines1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gary_Lopez:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matthew_Hall:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Federico_Zarfati:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yonatan_Zunger:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Emre_Kiciman:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.14720"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2403-14720,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Keegan Hines and Gary Lopez and Matthew Hall and Federico Zarfati and Yonatan Zunger and Emre Kiciman},\n  title={Defending Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks With Spotlighting},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2403.14720},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2403.14720}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs), while powerful, are built and trained to process a single text input. In common applications, multiple inputs can be processed by concatenating them together into a single stream of text. However, the LLM is unable to distinguish which sections of prompt belong to various input sources. Indirect prompt injection attacks take advantage of this vulnerability by embedding adversarial instructions into untrusted data being processed alongside user commands. Often, the LLM will mistake the adversarial instructions as user commands to be followed, creating a security vulnerability in the larger system. We introduce spotlighting, a family of prompt engineering techniques that can be used to improve LLMs' ability to distinguish among multiple sources of input. The key insight is to utilize transformations of an input to provide a reliable and continuous signal of its provenance. We evaluate spotlighting as a defense against indirect prompt injection attacks, and find that it is a robust defense that has minimal detrimental impact to underlying NLP tasks. Using GPT-family models, we find that spotlighting reduces the attack success rate from greater than {50}\\% to below {2}\\% in our experiments with minimal impact on task efficacy."},"title":{"value":"Defending Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks With Spotlighting"},"authors":{"value":["Keegan Hines","Gary Lopez","Matthew Hall","Federico Zarfati","Yonatan Zunger","Emre Kiciman"]}},"tmdate":1760144861920,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2403-14720"],"tcdate":1760144859752,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Keegan_Hines1"],"forum":"nku18PEYH1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":642243,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1760144861920,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"nku18PEYH1","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CAMLIS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3920/paper03.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CAMLIS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hines|defending_against_indirect_prompt_injection_attacks_with_spotlighting"},"authorids":{"value":["~Keegan_Hines1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gary_Lopez:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Matthew_Hall:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Federico_Zarfati:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yonatan_Zunger:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Emre_Kiciman:"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/camlis/HinesLHZZK24,\n  author={Keegan Hines and Gary Lopez and Matthew Hall and Federico Zarfati and Yonatan Zunger and Emre Kiciman},\n  title={Defending Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks With Spotlighting},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={48-62},\n  url={https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3920/paper03.pdf},\n  booktitle={CAMLIS},\n  crossref={conf/camlis/2024}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Defending Against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks With Spotlighting"},"authors":{"value":["Keegan Hines","Gary Lopez","Matthew Hall","Federico Zarfati","Yonatan Zunger","Emre Kiciman"]}},"tmdate":1760144858514,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/camlis/HinesLHZZK24"],"tcdate":1760144858354,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Keegan_Hines1"],"forum":"SFuu7IxPvO","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":642242,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1760144858514,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SFuu7IxPvO","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"USENIX Security Symposium"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|formalizing_and_benchmarking_prompt_injection_attacks_and_defenses"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yupei_Liu1","~Yuqi_Jia1","~Runpeng_Geng1","~Jinyuan_Jia2","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.12815"},"abstract":{"value":"A prompt injection attack aims to inject malicious instruction/data into the input of an LLM-Integrated Application\nsuch that it produces results as an attacker desires. Existing\nworks are limited to case studies. As a result, the literature\nlacks a systematic understanding of prompt injection attacks\nand their defenses. We aim to bridge the gap in this work.\nIn particular, we propose a framework to formalize prompt\ninjection attacks. Existing attacks are special cases in our\nframework. Moreover, based on our framework, we design a\nnew attack by combining existing ones. Using our framework,\nwe conduct a systematic evaluation on 5 prompt injection\nattacks and 10 defenses with 10 LLMs and 7 tasks. Our work\nprovides a common benchmark for quantitatively evaluating\nfuture prompt injection attacks and defenses. To facilitate\nresearch on this topic, we make our platform public at https:\n//github.com/liu00222/Open-Prompt-Injection"},"title":{"value":"Formalizing and Benchmarking Prompt Injection Attacks and Defenses"},"authors":{"value":["Yupei Liu","Yuqi Jia","Runpeng Geng","Jinyuan Jia","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1757079288117,"pdate":1723694400000,"tcdate":1757079288117,"writers":["~Yupei_Liu1","~Yuqi_Jia1","~Runpeng_Geng1","~Jinyuan_Jia2","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"],"signatures":["~Jinyuan_Jia2"],"forum":"qApV9smJkP","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":38838,"cdate":1757079288117,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1757079288117,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"qApV9smJkP","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.00348v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"hung|attention_tracker_detecting_prompt_injection_attacks_in_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kuo-Han_Hung:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ching-Yun_Ko:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ambrish_Rawat:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:I-Hsin_Chung:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Winston_H._Hsu:","~Pin-Yu_Chen3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.00348"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2411-00348,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Kuo-Han Hung and Ching-Yun Ko and Ambrish Rawat and I-Hsin Chung and Winston H. Hsu and Pin-Yu Chen},\n  title={Attention Tracker: Detecting Prompt Injection Attacks in LLMs},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2411.00348},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.00348}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized various domains but remain vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, where malicious inputs manipulate the model into ignoring original instructions and executing designated action. In this paper, we investigate the underlying mechanisms of these attacks by analyzing the attention patterns within LLMs. We introduce the concept of the distraction effect, where specific attention heads, termed important heads, shift focus from the original instruction to the injected instruction. Building on this discovery, we propose Attention Tracker, a training-free detection method that tracks attention patterns on instruction to detect prompt injection attacks without the need for additional LLM inference. Our method generalizes effectively across diverse models, datasets, and attack types, showing an AUROC improvement of up to 10.0% over existing methods, and performs well even on small LLMs. We demonstrate the robustness of our approach through extensive evaluations and provide insights into safeguarding LLM-integrated systems from prompt injection vulnerabilities."},"title":{"value":"Attention Tracker: Detecting Prompt Injection Attacks in LLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Kuo-Han Hung","Ching-Yun Ko","Ambrish Rawat","I-Hsin Chung","Winston H. Hsu","Pin-Yu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1756909987921,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2411-00348"],"tcdate":1756909955113,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Pin-Yu_Chen1"],"forum":"J2nPW7ZDCn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":620311,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1756909987921,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"J2nPW7ZDCn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NAACL (Findings) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-naacl.123.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/NAACL/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"hung|attention_tracker_detecting_prompt_injection_attacks_in_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kuo-Han_Hung:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ching-Yun_Ko:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ambrish_Rawat:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:I-Hsin_Chung:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Winston_H._Hsu:","~Pin-Yu_Chen3"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.findings-naacl.123"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/naacl/HungKRCHC25,\n  author={Kuo-Han Hung and Ching-Yun Ko and Ambrish Rawat and I-Hsin Chung and Winston H. Hsu and Pin-Yu Chen},\n  title={Attention Tracker: Detecting Prompt Injection Attacks in LLMs},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={2309-2322},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.findings-naacl.123},\n  booktitle={NAACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/naacl/2025f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have revolutionized various domains but remain vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, where malicious inputs manipulate the model into ignoring original instructions and executing designated action. In this paper, we investigate the underlying mechanisms of these attacks by analyzing the attention patterns within LLMs. We introduce the concept of the distraction effect, where specific attention heads, termed important heads, shift focus from the original instruction to the injected instruction. Building on this discovery, we propose Attention Tracker, a training-free detection method that tracks attention patterns on instruction to detect prompt injection attacks without the need for additional LLM inference. Our method generalizes effectively across diverse models, datasets, and attack types, showing an AUROC improvement of up to 10.0% over existing methods, and performs well even on small LLMs. We demonstrate the robustness of our approach through extensive evaluations and provide insights into safeguarding LLM-integrated systems from prompt injection vulnerabilities."},"title":{"value":"Attention Tracker: Detecting Prompt Injection Attacks in LLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Kuo-Han Hung","Ching-Yun Ko","Ambrish Rawat","I-Hsin Chung","Winston H. Hsu","Pin-Yu Chen"]}},"tmdate":1756909958668,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/naacl/HungKRCHC25"],"tcdate":1756909953127,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Pin-Yu_Chen1"],"forum":"6XsZ7ukaXw","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":620261,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1756909958668,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"6XsZ7ukaXw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ESORICS (1) 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-70879-4_6.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ESORICS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"piet|jatmo_prompt_injection_defense_by_taskspecific_finetuning"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Julien_Piet:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Maha_Alrashed:","~Chawin_Sitawarin1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sizhe_Chen:","~Zeming_Wei1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Elizabeth_Sun:","~Basel_Alomair1","~David_A._Wagner2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-70879-4_6"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/esorics/PietASCWSAW24,\n  author={Julien Piet and Maha Alrashed and Chawin Sitawarin and Sizhe Chen and Zeming Wei and Elizabeth Sun and Basel Alomair and David A. Wagner},\n  title={Jatmo: Prompt Injection Defense by Task-Specific Finetuning},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={105-124},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-70879-4_6},\n  booktitle={ESORICS (1)},\n  crossref={conf/esorics/2024-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are attracting significant research attention due to their instruction-following abilities, allowing users and developers to leverage LLMs for a variety of tasks. However, LLMs are vulnerable to prompt-injection attacks: a class of attacks that hijack the model’s instruction-following abilities, changing responses to prompts to undesired, possibly malicious ones. In this work, we introduce Jatmo , a method for generating task-specific models resilient to prompt-injection attacks. Jatmo leverages the fact that LLMs can only follow instructions once they have undergone instruction tuning. It harnesses a teacher instruction-tuned model to generate a task-specific dataset, which is then used to fine-tune a base model (i.e., a non-instruction-tuned model). Jatmo only needs a task prompt and a dataset of inputs for the task: it uses the teacher model to generate outputs. For situations with no pre-existing datasets, Jatmo can use a single example, or in some cases none at all, to produce a fully synthetic dataset. Our experiments on seven tasks show that Jatmo models provide similar quality of outputs on their specific task as standard LLMs, while being resilient to prompt injections. The best attacks succeeded in less than 0.5% of cases against our models, versus 87% success rate against GPT-3.5-Turbo. We release Jatmo at https://github.com/wagner-group/prompt-injection-defense."},"title":{"value":"Jatmo: Prompt Injection Defense by Task-Specific Finetuning"},"authors":{"value":["Julien Piet","Maha Alrashed","Chawin Sitawarin","Sizhe Chen","Zeming Wei","Elizabeth Sun","Basel Alomair","David A. Wagner"]}},"tmdate":1753812874313,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727453701050,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Chawin_Sitawarin1"],"forum":"DqILvCEytW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":87412,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1753812874313,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"DqILvCEytW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.14348v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|manipulating_multimodal_agents_via_crossmodal_prompt_injection"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Le_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zonghao_Ying:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Tianyuan_Zhang_0004:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Siyuan_Liang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shengshan_Hu:","~Mingchuan_Zhang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Aishan_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xianglong_Liu_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.14348"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-14348,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Le Wang and Zonghao Ying and Tianyuan Zhang and Siyuan Liang and Shengshan Hu and Mingchuan Zhang and Aishan Liu and Xianglong Liu},\n  title={Manipulating Multimodal Agents via Cross-Modal Prompt Injection},\n  year={2025},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1743465600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2504.14348},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.14348}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The emergence of multimodal large language models has redefined the agent paradigm by integrating language and vision modalities with external data sources, enabling agents to better interpret human instructions and execute increasingly complex tasks. However, in this work, we identify a critical yet previously overlooked security vulnerability in multimodal agents: cross-modal prompt injection attacks. To exploit this vulnerability, we propose CrossInject, a novel attack framework in which attackers embed adversarial perturbations across multiple modalities to align with target malicious content, allowing external instructions to hijack the agent's decision-making process and execute unauthorized tasks. Our approach consists of two key components. First, we introduce Visual Latent Alignment, where we optimize adversarial features to the malicious instructions in the visual embedding space based on a text-to-image generative model, ensuring that adversarial images subtly encode cues for malicious task execution. Subsequently, we present Textual Guidance Enhancement, where a large language model is leveraged to infer the black-box defensive system prompt through adversarial meta prompting and generate an malicious textual command that steers the agent's output toward better compliance with attackers' requests. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method outperforms existing injection attacks, achieving at least a +26.4% increase in attack success rates across diverse tasks. Furthermore, we validate our attack's effectiveness in real-world multimodal autonomous agents, highlighting its potential implications for safety-critical applications."},"title":{"value":"Manipulating Multimodal Agents via Cross-Modal Prompt Injection"},"authors":{"value":["Le Wang","Zonghao Ying","Tianyuan Zhang","Siyuan Liang","Shengshan Hu","Mingchuan Zhang","Aishan Liu","Xianglong Liu"]}},"tmdate":1753074522846,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1753074510873,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Mingchuan_Zhang2"],"forum":"witUvdKysH","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":578501,"cdate":1743465600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753074522846,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"witUvdKysH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2025 R2-FM Workshop Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fbcb5d2027f545230bcef9064cb3a44a8babeaa8.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Prompt injection defense","LLM-integrated application","AI security"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|defending_against_prompt_injection_with_a_few_defensivetokens"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sizhe_Chen1","~Yizhu_Wang1","~Nicholas_Carlini1","~Chawin_Sitawarin1","~David_Wagner3"]},"abstract":{"value":"When large language model (LLM) systems interact with external data to perform complex tasks, a new attack, namely prompt injection, becomes a significant threat. By injecting instructions into the data accessed by the system, the attacker is able to override the initial user task with an arbitrary task directed by the attacker. To secure the system, test-time defenses, e.g., defensive prompting, have been proposed for system developers to attain security only when needed in a flexible manner. However, they are much less effective than training-time defenses that change the model parameters. Motivated by this, we propose DefensiveToken, a test-time defense with prompt injection robustness comparable to training-time alternatives. DefensiveTokens are newly inserted as special tokens, whose embeddings are optimized for security. In security-sensitive cases, system developers can append a few DefensiveTokens before the LLM input to achieve security with a minimal utility drop. In scenarios where security is less of a concern, developers can simply skip DefensiveTokens; the LLM system remains the same as there is no defense, generating high-quality responses. Thus, DefensiveTokens, if released alongside the model, allow a flexible switch between the state-of-the-art (SOTA) utility and almost-SOTA security at test time. The code is available at https://github.com/Sizhe-Chen/DefensiveToken."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nchen2025defending,\ntitle={Defending Against Prompt Injection with a Few DefensiveTokens},\nauthor={Sizhe Chen and Yizhu Wang and Nicholas Carlini and Chawin Sitawarin and David Wagner},\nbooktitle={ICML 2025 Workshop on Reliable and Responsible Foundation Models},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=VAJQ8UblUo}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Defending Against Prompt Injection with a Few DefensiveTokens"},"authors":{"value":["Sizhe Chen","Yizhu Wang","Nicholas Carlini","Chawin Sitawarin","David Wagner"]}},"tmdate":1752262768299,"pdate":1751335657517,"tcdate":1747941197261,"writers":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/Submission23/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/Submission23/Authors"],"forum":"VAJQ8UblUo","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":23,"cdate":1747941197261,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM/Submission23/-/Camera_Ready_Submission"],"mdate":1752262768299,"odate":1751335846824,"domain":"ICML.cc/2025/Workshop/R2-FM","id":"VAJQ8UblUo","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CAMLIS 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3920/paper15.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/CAMLIS/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ayub|embeddingbased_classifiers_can_detect_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Md._Ahsan_Ayub:","~Subhabrata_Majumdar2"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/camlis/AyubM24,\n  author={Md. Ahsan Ayub and Subhabrata Majumdar},\n  title={Embedding-based classifiers can detect prompt injection attacks},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={257-268},\n  url={https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3920/paper15.pdf},\n  booktitle={CAMLIS},\n  crossref={conf/camlis/2024}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Embedding-based classifiers can detect prompt injection attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Md. Ahsan Ayub","Subhabrata Majumdar"]}},"tmdate":1747249059474,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747249052386,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Subhabrata_Majumdar2"],"forum":"0ueQiZ1WTR","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":465261,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747249059474,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0ueQiZ1WTR","version":2},{"content":{"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"zharmagambetov|aligning_llms_to_be_robust_against_prompt_injection"},"authorids":{"value":["~Arman_Zharmagambetov1"]},"html":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.05451"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) are becoming increasingly prevalent in modern software systems, interfacing between the user and the internet to assist with tasks that require advanced language understanding. To accomplish these tasks, the LLM often uses external data sources such as user documents, web retrieval, results from API calls, etc. This opens up new avenues for attackers to manipulate the LLM via prompt injection. Adversarial prompts can be carefully crafted and injected into external data sources to override the user's intended instruction and instead execute a malicious instruction. Prompt injection attacks constitute a major threat to LLM security, making the design and implementation of practical countermeasures of paramount importance. To this end, we show that alignment can be a powerful tool to make LLMs more robust against prompt injection. Our method -- SecAlign -- first builds an alignment dataset by simulating prompt injection attacks and constructing pairs of desirable and undesirable responses. Then, we apply existing alignment techniques to fine-tune the LLM to be robust against these simulated attacks. Our experiments show that SecAlign robustifies the LLM substantially with a negligible hurt on model utility. Moreover, SecAlign's protection generalizes to strong attacks unseen in training. Specifically, the success rate of state-of-the-art GCG-based prompt injections drops from 56% to 2% in Mistral-7B after our alignment process."},"title":{"value":"Aligning LLMs to Be Robust Against Prompt Injection"},"authors":{"value":["Arman Zharmagambetov"]}},"tmdate":1746031520899,"pdate":1728284400000,"tcdate":1746031520899,"writers":["~Arman_Zharmagambetov1"],"signatures":["~Arman_Zharmagambetov1"],"forum":"uiGpuG7wWV","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":35750,"cdate":1746031520899,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1746031520899,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"uiGpuG7wWV","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CAMLIS’24: Conference on Applied Machine Learning for Information Security, October 24–25, 2024, Arlington, VA"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"ayub|embeddingbased_classifiers_can_detect_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["ahsan.ayub@vumc.org","~Subhabrata_Majumdar2"]},"html":{"value":"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3920/paper15.pdf"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are seeing significant adoption in every type of organization due to their exceptional generative capabilities. However, LLMs are found to be vulnerable to various adversarial attacks, particularly prompt injection attacks, which trick them into producing harmful or inappropriate content. Adversaries execute such attacks by crafting malicious prompts to deceive the LLMs. In this paper, we propose a novel approach based on embedding-based Machine Learning (ML) classifiers to protect LLM-based applications against this severe threat. We leverage three commonly used embedding models to generate embeddings of malicious and benign prompts and utilize ML classifiers to predict whether an input prompt is malicious. Out of several traditional ML methods, we achieve the best performance with classifiers built using Random Forest and XGBoost. Our classifiers outperform state-of-the-art prompt injection classifiers available in open-source implementations, which use encoder-only neural networks."},"title":{"value":"Embedding-based classifiers can detect prompt injection attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Md Ahsan Ayub","Subhabrata Majumdar"]}},"tmdate":1745699248245,"pdate":1730185200000,"tcdate":1745699248245,"writers":["ahsan.ayub@vumc.org","~Subhabrata_Majumdar2"],"signatures":["~Subhabrata_Majumdar2"],"forum":"U9YQl11Bsb","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":35713,"cdate":1745699248245,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1745699248245,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"U9YQl11Bsb","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Intent Aware Framework to Benchmark Prompt  Guard Models on Over Defense and Difficult Prompt Attacks in LLM-based Applications"},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to BuildingTrust"},"keywords":{"value":["guardrail","prompt injection","security","llm application"]},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection remains a major security risk for large language models, enabling adversarial manipulation via crafted inputs. Various prompt guardrail models have been developed to mitigate this threat, yet their efficacy in intent-aware adversarial settings remains underexplored. Existing defenses lack robustness in intent-aware adversarial settings, often relying on static attack benchmarks. We introduce a novel intent-aware benchmarking framework that despite taking very few contextual examples as input, diversifies adversarial inputs and assesses over-defense tendencies. Our experiments reveal that current prompt injection guardrail models suffer from high false negatives in adversarial cases and excessive false positives in benign scenarios, highlighting critical limitations."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nanonymous2025benchmarking,\ntitle={Benchmarking Intent Awareness in Prompt Injection Guardrail Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=tURn0wNI3s}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Benchmarking Intent Awareness in Prompt Injection Guardrail Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/bdb849f3df4aa9a5b0636085fd175446c53c83fb.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/BuildingTrust/Rejected_Submission"},"track":{"value":"Tiny Paper Track (between 2 and 4 pages)"}},"tmdate":1741240351552,"tcdate":1739275015513,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/BuildingTrust","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/BuildingTrust/Submission136/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/BuildingTrust/Submission136/Authors"],"forum":"tURn0wNI3s","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":136,"cdate":1739275015513,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/BuildingTrust/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/BuildingTrust/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/BuildingTrust/-/Edit"],"mdate":1741240351552,"odate":1741240351538,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Workshop/BuildingTrust","id":"tURn0wNI3s","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"An interactive and dynamic benchmark for prompt injections with various data modality and threats analysis"},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["Prompt Injection Attack","Foundation Models","AI Agent"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Foundation Models (FMs) are increasingly integrated with external data sources and tools to handle complex tasks, forming FM-integrated systems with different modalities. However, such integration introduces new security vulnerabilities, especially when FMs interact dynamically with the system environments. One of the most critical threats is the prompt injection attack, where adversaries inject malicious instructions into the input environment, causing the model to deviate from user-intended behaviors. To advance the study of prompt injection vulnerabilities in FM-integrated systems, a comprehensive benchmark is essential. However, existing benchmarks fall short in two key areas: 1) they primarily focus on text-based modalities, lacking thorough analysis of diverse threats and attacks across more integrated modalities such as code, web pages, and vision; and 2) they rely on static test suites, failing to capture the dynamic, adversarial interplay between evolving attacks and defenses, as well as the interactive nature of agent-based environments. To bridge this gap, we propose the Prompt Injection Benchmark for FM-integrated Systems (FSPIB), which offers comprehensive coverage across various dimensions, including task modalities, threat categories, various attack and defense algorithms. Furthermore, FSPIB is interactive and dynamic, with evaluations conducted in interactive environments, and features a user-friendly front end that supports extensible attacks and defenses for ongoing research. By analyzing the performance of baseline prompt injection attacks and defenses, our benchmark highlights the prevalence of security vulnerabilities in FM-integrated systems and reveals the limited effectiveness of existing defense strategies, underscoring the urgent need for further research into prompt injection mitigation."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nwang2025prompt,\ntitle={Prompt Injection Benchmark for Foundation Model Integrated Systems},\nauthor={Jiongxiao Wang and Peiran Li and Siyuan Ma and Peiran Wang and Xiaogeng Liu and Jiashuo Sun and Yiming Liang and Tian Xia and Yu Wang and Weidi Luo and Chaowei Xiao},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=MsRdq0ePTR}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Prompt Injection Benchmark for Foundation Model Integrated Systems"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/1700541461a7b89df1a547d391a744635681a705.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|prompt_injection_benchmark_for_foundation_model_integrated_systems"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jiongxiao_Wang1","~Peiran_Li1","~Siyuan_Ma4","~Peiran_Wang1","~Xiaogeng_Liu1","~Jiashuo_Sun1","~Yiming_Liang3","~Tian_Xia17","~Yu_Wang43","~Weidi_Luo1","~Chaowei_Xiao2"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Jiongxiao Wang","Peiran Li","Siyuan Ma","Peiran Wang","Xiaogeng Liu","Jiashuo Sun","Yiming Liang","Tian Xia","Yu Wang","Weidi Luo","Chaowei Xiao"]}},"tmdate":1738735863482,"tcdate":1727487006846,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission12820/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission12820/Authors"],"forum":"MsRdq0ePTR","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":12820,"cdate":1727487006846,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission12820/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738735863482,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"MsRdq0ePTR","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We examines ICL in LLMs through the lens of cognitive neuroscience, revealing that LLMs, like humans, can experience cognitive overload that makes them vulnerable to adversarial prompts, and demonstrates how this can lead to effective jailbreaks."},"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2025"},"code_of_ethics":{"value":"I acknowledge that I and all co-authors of this work have read and commit to adhering to the ICLR Code of Ethics."},"keywords":{"value":["In Context Learning","Cognitive Overload","LLMs Safety","Prompt Injection Attack"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/8913ca58b4b4d97b7c606a46f1726c33af4fb246.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"submission_guidelines":{"value":"I certify that this submission complies with the submission instructions as described on https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2025/AuthorGuide."},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in performing tasks across various domains without needing explicit retraining. This capability, known as In-Context Learning (ICL), while impressive, exposes LLMs to a variety of adversarial prompts and jailbreaks that manipulate safety-trained LLMs into generating undesired or harmful output. In this paper, we propose a novel interpretation of ICL in LLMs through the lens of cognitive neuroscience, by drawing parallels between learning in human cognition with ICL. We applied the principles of Cognitive Load Theory in LLMs and empirically validate that similar to human cognition, LLMs also suffer from \\emph{cognitive overload}—a state where the demand on cognitive processing exceeds the available capacity of the model, leading to potential errors. Furthermore, we demonstrated how an attacker can exploit ICL to jailbreak LLMs through deliberately designed prompts that induce cognitive overload on LLMs, thereby compromising the safety mechanisms of LLMs. We empirically validate this threat model by crafting various cognitive overload prompts and show that advanced models such as GPT-4, Claude-3.5 Sonnet, Claude-3 OPUS, LLAMA-3-70B-Instruct, Gemini-1.0-Pro, and Gemini-1.5-Pro can be successfully jailbroken, with attack success rates of up to 99.99\\%. Our findings highlight critical vulnerabilities in LLMs and underscore the urgency of developing robust safeguards. We propose integrating insights from cognitive load theory into the design and evaluation of LLMs to better anticipate and mitigate the risks of adversarial attacks. By expanding our experiments to encompass a broader range of models and by highlighting vulnerabilities in LLMs' ICL, we aim to ensure the development of safer and more reliable AI systems."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nupadhayay2025cognitive,\ntitle={Cognitive Overload Attack: Prompt Injection for Long Context},\nauthor={Bibek Upadhayay and Vahid Behzadan and Amin Karbasi},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=L5fZHoaUCF}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Cognitive Overload Attack: Prompt Injection for Long Context"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/ea47233849fd3e99268ebde1dbd00d47498ee7f7.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"upadhayay|cognitive_overload_attack_prompt_injection_for_long_context"},"authorids":{"value":["~Bibek_Upadhayay1","~Vahid_Behzadan2","~Amin_Karbasi3"]},"anonymous_url":{"value":"I certify that there is no URL (e.g., github page) that could be used to find authors’ identity."},"no_acknowledgement_section":{"value":"I certify that there is no acknowledgement section in this submission for double blind review."},"authors":{"value":["Bibek Upadhayay","Vahid Behzadan","Amin Karbasi"]}},"tmdate":1738735792531,"tcdate":1727423070885,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission9447/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission9447/Authors"],"forum":"L5fZHoaUCF","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":9447,"cdate":1727423070885,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission9447/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/Submission9447/-/Rebuttal_Revision","ICLR.cc/2025/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738735792531,"odate":1728008565725,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2025/Conference","id":"L5fZHoaUCF","version":2},{"content":{"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6673e1657ab5cf244d3fdf7d7cf1b60ccf6a2f61.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Archive"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|struq_defending_against_prompt_injection_with_structured_queries"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sizhe_Chen1","~Julien_Piet1","~Chawin_Sitawarin1","~David_Wagner3"]},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) enable exciting LLM-integrated applications, which perform text-based tasks by utilizing their advanced language understanding capabilities. However, as LLMs have improved, so have the attacks against them. Prompt injection attacks are an important threat: they trick the model to deviate from the original application's instructions and instead follow user directives. These attacks rely on the LLM's ability to follow instructions and inability to separate the prompts and user data. We introduce structured queries, a general approach to tackle this problem. Structured queries separate prompts and data into two channels. We implement a system that supports structured queries. This system is made of (1) a secure front-end that formats a prompt and user data into a special format, and (2) a specially trained LLM that can produce high-quality outputs from these inputs. The LLM is trained using a novel fine-tuning strategy: we convert a base (non-instruction-tuned) LLM to a structured instruction-tuned model that will only follow instructions in the prompt portion of a query. To do so, we augment standard instruction tuning datasets with examples that also include instructions in the data portion of the query, and fine-tune the model to ignore these. Our system significantly improves resistance to prompt injection attacks, with little or no impact on utility. Our code is released at this https URL."},"title":{"value":"StruQ: Defending Against Prompt Injection with Structured Queries"},"authors":{"value":["Sizhe Chen","Julien Piet","Chawin Sitawarin","David Wagner"]}},"tmdate":1716457879417,"pdate":1707472260480,"tcdate":1716457879417,"writers":["~Sizhe_Chen1","~Julien_Piet1","~Chawin_Sitawarin1","~David_Wagner3"],"signatures":["~David_Wagner3"],"forum":"0zxWwDcl0e","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","number":26487,"cdate":1716457879417,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Archive/-/Direct_Upload"],"mdate":1716457879417,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Archive","id":"0zxWwDcl0e","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Our study reveals that user-customized ChatGPT models are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, exposing both system prompts and uploaded files, and stresses the need for enhanced security measures in AI customization."},"venue":{"value":"SeT LLM @ ICLR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/cb37713d72fa6601b10b8669f531b60c7359739e.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["large language model","security and privacy"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM"},"paperhash":{"value":"yu|assessing_prompt_injection_risks_in_200_custom_gpts"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jiahao_Yu1","~Yuhang_Wu8","~Dong_Shu1","~Mingyu_Jin1","~Sabrina_Yang1","~Xinyu_Xing3"]},"abstract":{"value":"In the rapidly evolving landscape of artificial intelligence, ChatGPT has been widely used in various applications. The new feature — customization of ChatGPT models by users to cater to specific needs has opened new frontiers in AI utility. However, this study reveals a significant security vulnerability inherent in these user-customized GPTs: prompt injection attacks. \nThrough comprehensive testing of over 200 user-designed GPT models via adversarial prompts, we demonstrate that these systems are susceptible to prompt injections. Through prompt injection, an adversary can not only extract the customized system prompts but also access the uploaded files. This paper provides a first-hand analysis of the prompt injection, alongside the evaluation of the possible mitigation of such attacks. Our findings underscore the urgent need for robust security frameworks in the design and deployment of customizable GPT models. The intent of this paper is to raise awareness and prompt action in the AI community, ensuring that the benefits of GPT customization do not come at the cost of compromised security and privacy."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nyu2024assessing,\ntitle={Assessing Prompt Injection Risks in 200+ Custom {GPT}s},\nauthor={Jiahao Yu and Yuhang Wu and Dong Shu and Mingyu Jin and Sabrina Yang and Xinyu Xing},\nbooktitle={ICLR 2024 Workshop on Secure and Trustworthy Large Language Models},\nyear={2024},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=XoXktpKp6x}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Assessing Prompt Injection Risks in 200+ Custom GPTs"},"authors":{"value":["Jiahao Yu","Yuhang Wu","Dong Shu","Mingyu Jin","Sabrina Yang","Xinyu Xing"]}},"tmdate":1713111800303,"pdate":1709535418084,"tcdate":1707692508353,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM/Submission27/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM/Submission27/Authors"],"forum":"XoXktpKp6x","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":27,"cdate":1707692508353,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM/Submission27/-/Revision"],"mdate":1713111800303,"odate":1713111800292,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2024/Workshop/SeT_LLM","id":"XoXktpKp6x","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.14368v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|is_your_prompt_safe_investigating_prompt_injection_attacks_against_opensource_llms"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jiawen_Wang:","~Pritha_Gupta1","~Ivan_Habernal1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Eyke_Hüllermeier:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.14368"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-14368,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jiawen Wang and Pritha Gupta and Ivan Habernal and Eyke Hüllermeier},\n  title={Is Your Prompt Safe? Investigating Prompt Injection Attacks Against Open-Source LLMs},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.14368},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.14368}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent studies demonstrate that Large Language Models (LLMs) are vulnerable to different prompt-based attacks, generating harmful content or sensitive information. Both closed-source and open-source LLMs are underinvestigated for these attacks. This paper studies effective prompt injection attacks against the $\\mathbf{14}$ most popular open-source LLMs on five attack benchmarks. Current metrics only consider successful attacks, whereas our proposed Attack Success Probability (ASP) also captures uncertainty in the model's response, reflecting ambiguity in attack feasibility. By comprehensively analyzing the effectiveness of prompt injection attacks, we propose a simple and effective hypnotism attack; results show that this attack causes aligned language models, including Stablelm2, Mistral, Openchat, and Vicuna, to generate objectionable behaviors, achieving around $90$% ASP. They also indicate that our ignore prefix attacks can break all $\\mathbf{14}$ open-source LLMs, achieving over $60$% ASP on a multi-categorical dataset. We find that moderately well-known LLMs exhibit higher vulnerability to prompt injection attacks, highlighting the need to raise public awareness and prioritize efficient mitigation strategies."},"title":{"value":"Is Your Prompt Safe? Investigating Prompt Injection Attacks Against Open-Source LLMs"},"authors":{"value":["Jiawen Wang","Pritha Gupta","Ivan Habernal","Eyke Hüllermeier"]}},"tmdate":1779810594079,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-14368"],"tcdate":1766470837536,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Ivan_Habernal1"],"forum":"EgLrGDlaJ9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":711423,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1779810594079,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"EgLrGDlaJ9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NAACL (Short Papers) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.naacl-short.21.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|defense_against_prompt_injection_attacks_via_mixture_of_encodings"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.naacl-short.21"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/naacl/ZhangSJXC25,\n  author={Ruiyi Zhang and David Sullivan and Kyle Jackson and Pengtao Xie and Mei Chen},\n  title={Defense against Prompt Injection Attacks via Mixture of Encodings},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={244-252},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.naacl-short.21},\n  booktitle={NAACL (Short Papers)},\n  crossref={conf/naacl/2025-2}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a dominant approach for a wide range of NLP tasks, with their access to external information further enhancing their capabilities. However, this introduces new vulnerabilities, known as prompt injection attacks, where external content embeds malicious instructions that manipulate the LLM’s output. Recently, the Base64 defense has been recognized as one of the most effective methods for reducing success rate of prompt injection attacks. Despite its efficacy, this method can degrade LLM performance on certain NLP tasks. To address this challenge, we propose a novel defense mechanism: mixture of encodings, which utilizes multiple character encodings, including Base64. Extensive experimental results show that our method achieves one of the lowest attack success rates under prompt injection attacks, while maintaining high performance across all NLP tasks, outperforming existing character encoding-based defense methods. This underscores the effectiveness of our mixture of encodings strategy for both safety and task performance metrics."},"title":{"value":"Defense against Prompt Injection Attacks via Mixture of Encodings"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Ruiyi Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"David Sullivan","username":""},{"fullname":"Kyle Jackson","username":""},{"fullname":"Pengtao Xie","username":"~Pengtao_Xie1"},{"fullname":"Mei Chen","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1792087234602,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/naacl/ZhangSJXC25"],"tcdate":1792087217192,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Pengtao_Xie3"],"forum":"L5gFtY82Q7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":189842,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792087234602,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"L5gFtY82Q7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.07467v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhang|defense_against_prompt_injection_attacks_via_mixture_of_encodings"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.07467"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-07467,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Ruiyi Zhang and David Sullivan and Kyle Jackson and Pengtao Xie and Mei Chen},\n  title={Defense against Prompt Injection Attacks via Mixture of Encodings},\n  year={2025},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1743465600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2504.07467},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.07467}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a dominant approach for a wide range of NLP tasks, with their access to external information further enhancing their capabilities. However, this introduces new vulnerabilities, known as prompt injection attacks, where external content embeds malicious instructions that manipulate the LLM's output. Recently, the Base64 defense has been recognized as one of the most effective methods for reducing success rate of prompt injection attacks. Despite its efficacy, this method can degrade LLM performance on certain NLP tasks. To address this challenge, we propose a novel defense mechanism: mixture of encodings, which utilizes multiple character encodings, including Base64. Extensive experimental results show that our method achieves one of the lowest attack success rates under prompt injection attacks, while maintaining high performance across all NLP tasks, outperforming existing character encoding-based defense methods. This underscores the effectiveness of our mixture of encodings strategy for both safety and task performance metrics."},"title":{"value":"Defense against Prompt Injection Attacks via Mixture of Encodings"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Ruiyi Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"David Sullivan","username":""},{"fullname":"Kyle Jackson","username":""},{"fullname":"Pengtao Xie","username":"~Pengtao_Xie1"},{"fullname":"Mei Chen","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1792087227749,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2504-07467"],"tcdate":1792087216892,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Pengtao_Xie3"],"forum":"HnZy0SUMOW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":189822,"cdate":1743465600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1792087227749,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"HnZy0SUMOW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.14005v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zou|pishield_detecting_prompt_injection_attacks_via_intrinsic_llm_features"},"authorids":{"value":["~Wei_Zou4","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yupei_Liu:","~Yanting_Wang2","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ying_Chen:","~Neil_Gong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyuan_Jia_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.14005"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2510-14005,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Wei Zou and Yupei Liu and Yanting Wang and Ying Chen and Neil Gong and Jinyuan Jia},\n  title={PIShield: Detecting Prompt Injection Attacks via Intrinsic LLM Features},\n  year={2025},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1759276800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2510.14005},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.14005}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"LLM-integrated applications are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, where an attacker contaminates the input to inject malicious prompts, causing the LLM to follow the attacker's intent instead of the original user's. Existing prompt injection detection methods often have sub-optimal performance and/or high computational overhead. In this work, we propose PIShield, a detection method that is both effective and efficient. Our key observation is that the internal representation of the final token in a prompt-extracted from a specific layer of the LLM, which we term the injection-critical layer-captures distinguishing features between clean and contaminated prompts. Leveraging this insight, we train a simple linear classifier on these internal representations using a labeled set of clean and contaminated prompts. We compare PIShield against 11 baselines across 5 diverse benchmark datasets and 8 prompt injection attacks. The results demonstrate that PIShield is both highly effective and efficient, substantially outperforming existing methods. Additionally, we show that PIShield resists strong adaptive attacks."},"title":{"value":"PIShield: Detecting Prompt Injection Attacks via Intrinsic LLM Features"},"authors":{"value":["Wei Zou","Yupei Liu","Yanting Wang","Ying Chen","Neil Gong","Jinyuan Jia"]}},"tmdate":1791956351490,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2510-14005"],"tcdate":1764166584391,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"NaQlOkshpU","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706381,"cdate":1759276800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791956351490,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"NaQlOkshpU","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.21642v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"huang|are_aiassisted_development_tools_immune_to_prompt_injection"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.21642"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2603-21642,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Charoes Huang and Xin Huang and Amin Milani Fard},\n  title={Are AI-assisted Development Tools Immune to Prompt Injection?},\n  year={2026},\n  month={March},\n  cdate={1772323200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2603.21642},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.21642}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection is listed as the number-one vulnerability class in the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications that can subvert LLM guardrails, disclose sensitive data, and trigger unauthorized tool use. Developers are rapidly adopting AI-assisted development tools built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP). However, their convenience comes with security risks, especially prompt-injection attacks delivered via tool-poisoning vectors. While prior research has studied prompt injection in LLMs, the security posture of real-world MCP clients remains underexplored. We present the first empirical analysis of prompt injection with the tool-poisoning vulnerability across seven widely used MCP clients: Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor, Cline, Continue, Gemini CLI, and Langflow. We identify their detection and mitigation mechanisms, as well as the coverage of security features, including static validation, parameter visibility, injection detection, user warnings, execution sandboxing, and audit logging. Our evaluation reveals significant disparities. While some clients, such as Claude Desktop, implement strong guardrails, others, such as Cursor, exhibit high susceptibility to cross-tool poisoning, hidden parameter exploitation, and unauthorized tool invocation. We further provide actionable guidance for MCP implementers and the software engineering community seeking to build secure AI-assisted development workflows."},"title":{"value":"Are AI-assisted Development Tools Immune to Prompt Injection?"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Charoes Huang","username":""},{"fullname":"Xin Huang","username":""},{"fullname":"Amin Milani Fard","username":"~Amin_Milani_Fard1"}]}},"tmdate":1791913707970,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2603-21642"],"tcdate":1791913702427,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Amin_Milani_Fard1"],"forum":"JoDbGJotXp","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":179783,"cdate":1772323200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791913707970,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"JoDbGJotXp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.102.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|transferable_direct_prompt_injection_via_activationguided_mcmc_sampling"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.102"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/LiZZWHXW25,\n  author={Minghui Li and Hao Zhang and Yechao Zhang and Wei Wan and Shengshan Hu and Pei Xiaobing and Jing Wang},\n  title={Transferable Direct Prompt Injection via Activation-Guided MCMC Sampling},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={1966-1978},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.102},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Direct Prompt Injection (DPI) attacks pose a critical security threat to Large Language Models (LLMs) due to their low barrier of execution and high potential damage. To address the impracticality of existing white-box/gray-box methods and the poor transferability of black-box methods, we propose an activations-guided prompt injection attack framework. We first construct an Energy-based Model (EBM) using activations from a surrogate model to evaluate the quality of adversarial prompts. Guided by the trained EBM, we employ the token-level Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling to adaptively optimize adversarial prompts, thereby enabling gradient-free black-box attacks. Experimental results demonstrate our superior cross-model transferability, achieving 49.6% attack success rate (ASR) across five mainstream LLMs and 34.6% improvement over human-crafted prompts, and maintaining 36.6% ASR on unseen task scenarios. Interpretability analysis reveals a correlation between activations and attack effectiveness, highlighting the critical role of semantic patterns in transferable vulnerability exploitation."},"title":{"value":"Transferable Direct Prompt Injection via Activation-Guided MCMC Sampling"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Minghui Li","username":"~Minghui_Li2"},{"fullname":"Hao Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yechao Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Wei Wan","username":"~Wei_Wan2"},{"fullname":"Shengshan Hu","username":""},{"fullname":"Pei Xiaobing","username":""},{"fullname":"Jing Wang","username":"~Jing_Wang63"}]}},"tmdate":1791907196712,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/LiZZWHXW25"],"tcdate":1784534685283,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Wei_Wan2"],"forum":"yQFrjsCKG9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":67035,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1791907196712,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"yQFrjsCKG9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.07617v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|transferable_direct_prompt_injection_via_activationguided_mcmc_sampling"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.07617"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2509-07617,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Minghui Li and Hao Zhang and Yechao Zhang and Wei Wan and Shengshan Hu and Pei Xiaobing and Jing Wang},\n  title={Transferable Direct Prompt Injection via Activation-Guided MCMC Sampling},\n  year={2025},\n  month={September},\n  cdate={1756684800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2509.07617},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.07617}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Direct Prompt Injection (DPI) attacks pose a critical security threat to Large Language Models (LLMs) due to their low barrier of execution and high potential damage. To address the impracticality of existing white-box/gray-box methods and the poor transferability of black-box methods, we propose an activations-guided prompt injection attack framework. We first construct an Energy-based Model (EBM) using activations from a surrogate model to evaluate the quality of adversarial prompts. Guided by the trained EBM, we employ the token-level Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling to adaptively optimize adversarial prompts, thereby enabling gradient-free black-box attacks. Experimental results demonstrate our superior cross-model transferability, achieving 49.6% attack success rate (ASR) across five mainstream LLMs and 34.6% improvement over human-crafted prompts, and maintaining 36.6% ASR on unseen task scenarios. Interpretability analysis reveals a correlation between activations and attack effectiveness, highlighting the critical role of semantic patterns in transferable vulnerability exploitation."},"title":{"value":"Transferable Direct Prompt Injection via Activation-Guided MCMC Sampling"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Minghui Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Hao Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yechao Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Wei Wan","username":"~Wei_Wan2"},{"fullname":"Shengshan Hu","username":""},{"fullname":"Pei Xiaobing","username":""},{"fullname":"Jing Wang","username":"~Jing_Wang63"}]}},"tmdate":1791907196674,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2509-07617"],"tcdate":1784534685227,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Wei_Wan2"],"forum":"LDQG3gTvVM","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":67034,"cdate":1756684800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1791907196674,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"LDQG3gTvVM","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"An agent that can order DNA can be injected into ordering a construct other than the one planned and screened. A plan-bound monitor blocked this in 30 of 30 trials where a frozen rule baseline blocked none."},"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2026 Workshop AgenticLS Poster"},"keywords":{"value":["prompt injection","LLM agents","biosecurity","DNA synthesis screening","runtime monitoring","agent security","order integrity","AI safety"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS"},"paperhash":{"value":"rajeev|order_integrity_for_agentic_dna_synthesis_under_prompt_injection"},"abstract":{"value":"Laboratories are beginning to delegate DNA synthesis ordering to language-model agents. Screening synthesized sequences against sequences of concern is the established safeguard at that step, and because it validates a particular artifact, it assumes that the sequence under review is the sequence that will be made. An agent that reads untrusted text does not satisfy that assumption, because an indirect prompt injection, in which instructions hidden in data the agent reads are followed as though they came from the user, can cause it to order a construct other than the one that was planned and screened. We call this a failure of order integrity. Using a bioinformatics agent with simulated ordering, we compared four conditions over a complete grid of 676 episodes, consisting of no monitor, a static rule baseline, plan-bound monitoring, and plan-bound monitoring whose monitor also reads untrusted content. Plan-bound monitoring requires the agent to commit to a plan before it reads any untrusted data, after which a separate monitor that never sees that data blocks any action departing from the plan. On the redirected order the undefended agent ordered the attacker's construct in 27 of 30 trials and the rule baseline did so in 22 of 30, whereas plan-bound monitoring blocked the order in all 30 trials, with an exact McNemar p of 4.8e-7 against the baseline. The redirected order evades the baseline because it satisfies every static rule, including the requirement that a screen precede an order. On attacks that a static rule can express the two defenses are indistinguishable, and on benign episodes both block the same nine of 183 calls with no loss of task completion. Fabricated results defeat all four conditions equally."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nrajeev2026order,\ntitle={Order Integrity for Agentic {DNA} Synthesis Under Prompt Injection},\nauthor={Vishnu Rajeev and Anatole Seychal},\nbooktitle={NeurIPS 2026 Agentic AI for Biological Discovery Workshop},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=jhSn7fQiuu}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Order Integrity for Agentic DNA Synthesis Under Prompt Injection"},"authors":{"value":[{"username":"~Vishnu_Rajeev1","fullname":"Vishnu Rajeev","institutions":[{"name":"University of California, Berkeley","domain":"berkeley.edu","country":"US"}]},{"username":"~Anatole_Seychal1","fullname":"Anatole Seychal","institutions":[{"name":"University of California, Berkeley","domain":"math.berkeley.edu","country":"US"}]}]}},"nonreaders":[],"tmdate":1791756046974,"pdate":1790885859320,"tcdate":1790489111706,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS/Submission149/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS/Submission149/Authors"],"forum":"jhSn7fQiuu","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":149,"cdate":1790489111706,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS/Submission149/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1791756046974,"odate":1790885859320,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2026/Workshop/AgenticLS","id":"jhSn7fQiuu","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["retrieval-augmented generation","prompt injection","RAG security"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems are vulnerable to prompt injection\nattacks that insert malicious documents into their knowledge databases. Many existing RAG defenses intervene only after the database has already been contaminated, for example by hardening the language model or filtering retrieved content at query time. These approaches have complementary but distinct limitations: model-level defenses are tied to the serving model, while query-time defenses repeatedly incur security processing during inference; moreover, neither directly removes the underlying contamination from the knowledge database. We therefore explore a complementary perspective: treating the knowledge database itself as a proactive security layer and sanitizing it before retrieval and generation.\nWe introduce CleanBase, a training-free offline sanitizer that exploits cross-document semantic relationships in the knowledge database. CleanBase constructs a $k$-nearest-neighbor graph over documents, prunes weak edges using a data-adaptive threshold, and removes documents participating in tightly connected groups. It requires neither future user queries nor access to the serving language model, and introduces no additional CleanBase computation per query after sanitization.\nAcross six knowledge databases and nine poisoning attacks, CleanBase reduces mean attack success rate from 69.9\\% to 9.1\\% at the evaluated operating settings while largely preserving aggregate RAG utility. We further characterize its limitations under low-cardinality and detector-aware diversified attacks. These results demonstrate the promise of database-level sanitization as a complementary defense layer for RAG systems. Our source code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/CleanBase-7762."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026cleanbase,\ntitle={CleanBase: Detecting Multi-Document Prompt Injection in {RAG} Databases},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=OtonxkK5L3},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CleanBase: Detecting Multi-Document Prompt Injection in RAG Databases"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fe24db179d2180cb54ce44d948c80d2f061feb8d.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791234853498,"tcdate":1789791715264,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission53940/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission53940/Authors"],"forum":"OtonxkK5L3","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":53940,"cdate":1789791715264,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission53940/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing"],"mdate":1791234853498,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"OtonxkK5L3","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"LLM agents can encode source role before that representation becomes behaviorally actionable, while effective interventions depend on where they are applied and transfer poorly across channels."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["indirect prompt injection","LLM agents","mechanistic interpretability","activation patching","representation probing","causal intervention"]},"supplementary_material":{"value":"/attachment/bb98db1964898d10c1091c24f98fa31c49eda6ed.zip"},"primary_area":{"value":"interpretability and explainable AI"},"abstract":{"value":"Indirect prompt injection causes LLM agents to follow commands embedded in external data. A probe may distinguish instructions from data without identifying a state edit that changes the next action. We study this gap through counterfactual role probes, component-wise activation patching, and separate interventions on AgentDojo trajectories. Role decoding survives changes in content and format. In controlled Qwen tests, it precedes strong tool-choice effects from patches along an independently estimated role direction. On AgentDojo, directions estimated from hijacked and resisted training trajectories reduce attack success at pre-action and injected-span positions, but have little effect at random positions. In longer Qwen trajectories, single-position edits become less effective at later layers; span-wide and repeated edits reduce attack success on the same evaluation set. Removing the learned channel subspace preserves role decoding, yet effective intervention directions transfer poorly across the tested channels. These findings distinguish a readable role signal from an effective behavioral intervention: depth matters in controlled tool choice, while position and context also matter in attack trajectories."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026readable,\ntitle={Readable Before Actionable: Causal Tracing of Indirect Prompt Injection},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=xxyC5WkjEh},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Readable Before Actionable: Causal Tracing of Indirect Prompt Injection"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6544de15af1304c50dc68aa01e82ae3a74ccbf79.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791232901623,"tcdate":1789724231959,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission40595/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission40595/Authors"],"forum":"xxyC5WkjEh","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":40595,"cdate":1789724231959,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission40595/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791232901623,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"xxyC5WkjEh","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"A Red Teaming Agent to discover Prompt Injection vulnerability automatically."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Red team","prompt injection","agent safety","agent security"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"LLM agents retrieve untrusted content and act through tools, creating indirect prompt-injection risks that can cause unauthorized actions or persistent state changes. Existing automated red-teaming largely optimizes payloads for pre-specified scenarios, leaving latent vulnerabilities across the agent's behavior space unexplored. We present ASPRIE, an Agentic Safety \\& Prompt Injection Red-teaming Engine for open-ended, behavior-level vulnerability discovery. ASPIRE maintains an evolving Agent Security Behavior Graph and uses complementary Explore and Exploit experts to discover, verify, and generalize consequence-centric tests. Trajectory evidence updates the graph and diagnoses partial or failed attempts, while cross-run memory transfers useful search strategies. Experiments on AgentDojo and AgentDyn show that ASPIRE substantially expands coverage across consequences, injection methods, environments, and behavior paths while maintaining strong attack success."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026aspire,\ntitle={{ASPIRE}: Agentic Safety and Prompt Injection Red-teaming Engine},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=9QOtWOZmJW},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"ASPIRE: Agentic Safety and Prompt Injection Red-teaming Engine"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/fad9b27c59df7495cadb1e963a4c002d72e25688.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791230585058,"tcdate":1789576240495,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26250/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26250/Authors"],"forum":"9QOtWOZmJW","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":26250,"cdate":1789576240495,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission26250/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791230585058,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"9QOtWOZmJW","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["Prompt Injection Attack","LLM Agent","Safety Alignment"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly act as autonomous agents, interacting with tools and environments to execute multi-step tasks. Untrusted observations expose them to indirect prompt injection (IPI), where malicious instructions in tool outputs redirect execution away from the user’s goal. Existing defenses incur latency and utility costs through inference-time filtering or verification, or fine-tune on finite attack templates that may not generalize to adaptive injections.\n\nWe introduce SecStep, a training-based defense that embeds action backtracking into the agent policy. SecStep enables agents to audit observations, detect conflicts with trusted user goals, isolate malicious instructions, and backtrack from contaminated paths to resume safe execution. To optimize these behaviors in long-horizon workflows, we further propose SecStep-RL, which combines implicit step-level rewards derived from trajectory-level preferences with global outcome rewards, providing dense supervision without costly process annotations. Our theoretical analysis shows that trajectory-level preference optimization can induce step-level rewards. Evaluations on AgentDojo and Agent Security Bench across open-weight and closed-source models demonstrate an improved security-utility trade-off. SecStep-RL achieves near-zero attack success rates under static attacks and 5.83\\% and 2.80\\% on Qwen3-4B and Llama-3.1-8B, respectively, under defense-aware adaptive attacks."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026secstep,\ntitle={SecStep: Guarding Agents Against Prompt Injection via Action Backtracking},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=psmPjQmrZ4},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"SecStep: Guarding Agents Against Prompt Injection via Action Backtracking"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/dc555460540ea5b52d82ddfbeaccade8e997fdc5.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791230221916,"tcdate":1789544957082,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission23993/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission23993/Authors"],"forum":"psmPjQmrZ4","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":23993,"cdate":1789544957082,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission23993/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791230221916,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"psmPjQmrZ4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 regular"},"keywords":{"value":["Indirect Prompt injection","LLM Agent Security","Attribution-based Defense"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nkim2026causalarmor,\ntitle={CausalArmor: Efficient Indirect Prompt Injection Guardrails via Causal Attribution},\nauthor={Minbeom Kim and Mihir Parmar and Phillip Wallis and Lesly Miculicich and Kyomin Jung and Krishnamurthy Dj Dvijotham and Long T. Le and Tomas Pfister},\nbooktitle={Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=fE4urn9hyM}\n}"},"title":{"value":"CausalArmor: Efficient Indirect Prompt Injection Guardrails via Causal Attribution"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|causalarmor_efficient_indirect_prompt_injection_guardrails_via_causal_attribution"},"originally_submitted_PDF":{"value":"/pdf/182e3a7e3ebfa303afdfe9e57e067199de0bb652.pdf"},"TLDR":{"value":"CausalArmor efficiently defends against Indirect Prompt Injection by sanitizing only when causal attribution shows that untrusted inputs overriding the user’s intent."},"primary_area":{"value":"social_aspects->security"},"abstract":{"value":"AI agents equipped with tool-calling capabilities are susceptible to *Indirect Prompt Injection* (IPI) attacks. In this attack scenario, malicious commands hidden within *untrusted* content trick the agent into performing unauthorized actions. Existing defenses can reduce attack success but often suffer from the ***over-defense dilemma***: they deploy expensive, *always-on* sanitization regardless of actual threat, thereby degrading utility and latency even in benign scenarios.\nWe revisit IPI through a causal ablation perspective: a successful injection manifests as a *dominance shift* where the user request no longer provides decisive support for the agent's privileged action, while a particular untrusted segment, such as a retrieved document or tool output, provides disproportionate attributable influence.\nBased on this signature, we propose **CausalArmor**, a selective defense framework that (i) computes lightweight, leave-one-out ablation-based attributions at privileged decision points, and (ii) triggers targeted sanitization only when an untrusted segment dominates the user intent. Additionally, CausalArmor employs *retroactive Chain-of-Thought masking* to prevent the agent from acting on ``poisoned\" reasoning traces.\nWe present a theoretical analysis showing that sanitization based on attribution margins conditionally yields an exponentially small upper bound on the probability of selecting malicious actions.\nExperiments on AgentDojo and DoomArena demonstrate that CausalArmor matches the security of aggressive defenses while improving explainability and preserving utility and latency of AI agents."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/af39bcb39577bb84ddc0cd270e2bc7d369e93d03.pdf"},"lay_summary":{"value":"AI agents can now browse websites, read emails, and call external tools on behalf of users. This makes them useful, but also creates a new security risk: a webpage, email, or tool output can hide malicious instructions that trick the agent into sending data, writing files, or taking other unauthorized actions. Existing defenses often try to stay safe by checking or cleaning everything the agent sees, but this can slow the agent down and sometimes remove useful information even when there is no attack.\n\nWe propose CausalArmor, a guardrail that intervenes only at high-risk moments. When an agent is about to take an important action, CausalArmor asks a simple question: is this action mainly supported by the user’s request, or by some untrusted content the agent recently read? If untrusted content appears to be driving the action, CausalArmor cleans only that suspicious part and removes any earlier reasoning that may have been contaminated by the attack before the agent tries again. On two agent-security benchmarks, this selective approach blocks almost all successful prompt-injection attacks while keeping normal task performance and speed close to an undefended agent. This suggests that safer AI agents do not always need heavier defenses; they can instead use targeted checks that explain where malicious influence is coming from."},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference"},"authorids":{"value":["~Minbeom_Kim2","~Mihir_Parmar1","~Phillip_Wallis1","~Lesly_Miculicich1","~Kyomin_Jung1","~Krishnamurthy_Dj_Dvijotham1","~Long_T._Le2","~Tomas_Pfister1"]},"authors":{"value":["Minbeom Kim","Mihir Parmar","Phillip Wallis","Lesly Miculicich","Kyomin Jung","Krishnamurthy Dj Dvijotham","Long T. Le","Tomas Pfister"]}},"tmdate":1790067310374,"pdate":1777576262561,"tcdate":1768659442451,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission6614/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission6614/Authors"],"forum":"fE4urn9hyM","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":6614,"cdate":1768659442451,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission6614/-/Reciprocal_Reviewing_Correction","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission6614/-/Full_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Conference/Submission6614/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1790067310374,"odate":1782341922570,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Conference","id":"fE4urn9hyM","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"ma|rope_routed_origin_policy_enforcement_against_indirect_prompt_injection"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.27496"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2608-27496,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Xinhang Ma and Chaowei Xiao and William Yeoh and Ning Zhang and Yevgeniy Vorobeychik},\n  title={ROPE: Routed Origin Policy Enforcement against Indirect Prompt Injection},\n  year={2026},\n  month={August},\n  cdate={1785542400000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2608.27496},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2608.27496}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"ROPE: Routed Origin Policy Enforcement against Indirect Prompt Injection"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Xinhang Ma","username":"~Xinhang_Ma1"},{"fullname":"Chaowei Xiao","username":""},{"fullname":"William Yeoh","username":""},{"fullname":"Ning Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yevgeniy Vorobeychik","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1789874062590,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2608-27496"],"tcdate":1789874062332,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Xinhang_Ma1"],"forum":"yDWqsofKN5","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":151738,"cdate":1785542400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1789874062590,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"yDWqsofKN5","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2602.07918v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"kim|causalarmor_efficient_indirect_prompt_injection_guardrails_via_causal_attribution"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07918"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2602-07918,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Minbeom Kim and Mihir Parmar and Phillip Wallis and Lesly Miculicich and Kyomin Jung and Krishnamurthy Dj Dvijotham and Long T. Le and Tomas Pfister},\n  title={CausalArmor: Efficient Indirect Prompt Injection Guardrails via Causal Attribution},\n  year={2026},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1769904000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2602.07918},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.07918}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"AI agents equipped with tool-calling capabilities are susceptible to Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI) attacks. In this attack scenario, malicious commands hidden within untrusted content trick the agent into performing unauthorized actions. Existing defenses can reduce attack success but often suffer from the over-defense dilemma: they deploy expensive, always-on sanitization regardless of actual threat, thereby degrading utility and latency even in benign scenarios. We revisit IPI through a causal ablation perspective: a successful injection manifests as a dominance shift where the user request no longer provides decisive support for the agent's privileged action, while a particular untrusted segment, such as a retrieved document or tool output, provides disproportionate attributable influence. Based on this signature, we propose CausalArmor, a selective defense framework that (i) computes lightweight, leave-one-out ablation-based attributions at privileged decision points, and (ii) triggers targeted sanitization only when an untrusted segment dominates the user intent. Additionally, CausalArmor employs retroactive Chain-of-Thought masking to prevent the agent from acting on ``poisoned'' reasoning traces. We present a theoretical analysis showing that sanitization based on attribution margins conditionally yields an exponentially small upper bound on the probability of selecting malicious actions. Experiments on AgentDojo and DoomArena demonstrate that CausalArmor matches the security of aggressive defenses while improving explainability and preserving utility and latency of AI agents."},"title":{"value":"CausalArmor: Efficient Indirect Prompt Injection Guardrails via Causal Attribution"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Minbeom Kim","username":"~Minbeom_Kim2"},{"fullname":"Mihir Parmar","username":""},{"fullname":"Phillip Wallis","username":""},{"fullname":"Lesly Miculicich","username":"~Lesly_Miculicich1"},{"fullname":"Kyomin Jung","username":""},{"fullname":"Krishnamurthy Dj Dvijotham","username":""},{"fullname":"Long T. Le","username":""},{"fullname":"Tomas Pfister","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1786034551362,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2602-07918"],"tcdate":1785391108466,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Minbeom_Kim2"],"forum":"REoShYJpuz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":112088,"cdate":1769904000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Authorship_Claim"],"mdate":1786034551362,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"REoShYJpuz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"SP 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11023178/11023253/11023476.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/SP/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|datasentinel_a_gametheoretic_detection_of_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yupei_Liu:","~Yuqi_Jia1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyuan_Jia_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dawn_Song:","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SP61157.2025.00250"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/sp/LiuJ0SG25,\n  author={Yupei Liu and Yuqi Jia and Jinyuan Jia and Dawn Song and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={DataSentinel: A Game-Theoretic Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={2190-2208},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/SP61157.2025.00250},\n  booktitle={SP},\n  crossref={conf/sp/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"LLM-integrated applications and agents are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, where an attacker injects prompts into their inputs to induce attacker-desired outputs. A detection method aims to determine whether a given input is contaminated by an injected prompt. However, existing detection methods have limited effectiveness against state-of-the-art attacks, let alone adaptive ones. In this work, we propose DataSentinel, a game-theoretic method to detect prompt injection attacks. Specifically, DataSentinel fine-tunes an LLM to detect inputs contaminated with injected prompts that are strategically adapted to evade detection. We formulate this as a minimax optimization problem, with the objective of fine-tuning the LLM to detect strong adaptive attacks. Furthermore, we propose a gradient-based method to solve the minimax optimization problem by alternating between the inner max and outer min problems. Our evaluation results on multiple benchmark datasets and LLMs show that DataSentinel effectively detects both existing and adaptive prompt injection attacks. Our code and data are available at: https://github.com/liu00222/Open-Prompt-Injection."},"title":{"value":"DataSentinel: A Game-Theoretic Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Yupei Liu","Yuqi Jia","Jinyuan Jia","Dawn Song","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1785854938153,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/sp/LiuJ0SG25"],"tcdate":1764166583645,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"vulGQTgPDK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706377,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785854938153,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"vulGQTgPDK","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.18333v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"jia|a_critical_evaluation_of_defenses_against_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yuqi_Jia1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zedian_Shao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yupei_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyuan_Jia_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dawn_Song:","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.18333"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2505-18333,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuqi Jia and Zedian Shao and Yupei Liu and Jinyuan Jia and Dawn Song and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={A Critical Evaluation of Defenses against Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2025},\n  month={May},\n  cdate={1746057600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2505.18333},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.18333}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, and several defenses have recently been proposed, often claiming to mitigate these attacks successfully. However, we argue that existing studies lack a principled approach to evaluating these defenses. In this paper, we argue the need to assess defenses across two critical dimensions: (1) effectiveness, measured against both existing and adaptive prompt injection attacks involving diverse target and injected prompts, and (2) general-purpose utility, ensuring that the defense does not compromise the foundational capabilities of the LLM. Our critical evaluation reveals that prior studies have not followed such a comprehensive evaluation methodology. When assessed using this principled approach, we show that existing defenses are not as successful as previously reported. This work provides a foundation for evaluating future defenses and guiding their development. Our code and data are available at: https://github.com/PIEval123/PIEval."},"title":{"value":"A Critical Evaluation of Defenses against Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Yuqi Jia","Zedian Shao","Yupei Liu","Jinyuan Jia","Dawn Song","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1785854938128,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2505-18333"],"tcdate":1764166583398,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"P8CO9mWMTi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706366,"cdate":1746057600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785854938128,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"P8CO9mWMTi","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.11358v4"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|datasentinel_a_gametheoretic_detection_of_prompt_injection_attacks"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yupei_Liu:","~Yuqi_Jia1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jinyuan_Jia_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dawn_Song:","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.11358"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-11358,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yupei Liu and Yuqi Jia and Jinyuan Jia and Dawn Song and Neil Zhenqiang Gong},\n  title={DataSentinel: A Game-Theoretic Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks},\n  year={2025},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1743465600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2504.11358},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.11358}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"LLM-integrated applications and agents are vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, where an attacker injects prompts into their inputs to induce attacker-desired outputs. A detection method aims to determine whether a given input is contaminated by an injected prompt. However, existing detection methods have limited effectiveness against state-of-the-art attacks, let alone adaptive ones. In this work, we propose DataSentinel, a game-theoretic method to detect prompt injection attacks. Specifically, DataSentinel fine-tunes an LLM to detect inputs contaminated with injected prompts that are strategically adapted to evade detection. We formulate this as a minimax optimization problem, with the objective of fine-tuning the LLM to detect strong adaptive attacks. Furthermore, we propose a gradient-based method to solve the minimax optimization problem by alternating between the inner max and outer min problems. Our evaluation results on multiple benchmark datasets and LLMs show that DataSentinel effectively detects both existing and adaptive prompt injection attacks."},"title":{"value":"DataSentinel: A Game-Theoretic Detection of Prompt Injection Attacks"},"authors":{"value":["Yupei Liu","Yuqi Jia","Jinyuan Jia","Dawn Song","Neil Zhenqiang Gong"]}},"tmdate":1785854938094,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2504-11358"],"tcdate":1764166583483,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"h0yq3BSbf9","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706364,"cdate":1743465600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1785854938094,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"h0yq3BSbf9","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.17383v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"ling|physical_prompt_injection_attacks_on_large_visionlanguage_models"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.17383"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2601-17383,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Chen Ling and Kai Hu and Hangcheng Liu and Xingshuo Han and Tianwei Zhang and Changhai Ou},\n  title={Physical Prompt Injection Attacks on Large Vision-Language Models},\n  year={2026},\n  month={January},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2601.17383},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.17383}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) are increasingly deployed in real-world intelligent systems for perception and reasoning in open physical environments. While LVLMs are known to be vulnerable to prompt injection attacks, existing methods either require access to input channels or depend on knowledge of user queries, assumptions that rarely hold in practical deployments. We propose the first Physical Prompt Injection Attack (PPIA), a black-box, query-agnostic attack that embeds malicious typographic instructions into physical objects perceivable by the LVLM. PPIA requires no access to the model, its inputs, or internal pipeline, and operates solely through visual observation. It combines offline selection of highly recognizable and semantically effective visual prompts with strategic environment-aware placement guided by spatiotemporal attention, ensuring that the injected prompts are both perceivable and influential on model behavior. We evaluate PPIA across 10 state-of-the-art LVLMs in both simulated and real-world settings on tasks including visual question answering, planning, and navigation, PPIA achieves attack success rates up to 98%, with strong robustness under varying physical conditions such as distance, viewpoint, and illumination. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/2023cghacker/Physical-Prompt-Injection-Attack."},"title":{"value":"Physical Prompt Injection Attacks on Large Vision-Language Models"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Chen Ling","username":""},{"fullname":"Kai Hu","username":""},{"fullname":"Hangcheng Liu","username":""},{"fullname":"Xingshuo Han","username":""},{"fullname":"Tianwei Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Changhai Ou","username":"~Changhai_Ou1"}]}},"tmdate":1784971837153,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2601-17383"],"tcdate":1784971829971,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Changhai_Ou1"],"forum":"xEQZ8wBbWT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":104178,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784971837153,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"xEQZ8wBbWT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.23675v3"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"xie|queryipi_queryagnostic_indirect_prompt_injection_on_coding_agents"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.23675"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2510-23675,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yuchong Xie and Zesen Liu and Mingyu Luo and Zhixiang Zhang and Kaikai Zhang and Zongjie Li and Ping Chen and Shuai Wang and Dongdong She},\n  title={QueryIPI: Query-agnostic Indirect Prompt Injection on Coding Agents},\n  year={2025},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1759276800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2510.23675},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.23675}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Modern coding agents integrated into IDEs orchestrate powerful tools and high-privilege system access, creating a high-stakes attack surface. Prior work on Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI) is mainly query-specific, requiring particular user queries as triggers and leading to poor generalizability. We propose query-agnostic IPI, a new attack paradigm that reliably executes malicious payloads under arbitrary user queries. Our key insight is that malicious payloads should leverage the invariant prompt context (i.e., system prompt and tool descriptions) rather than variant user queries. We present QueryIPI, an automated framework that uses tool descriptions as optimizable payloads and refines them via iterative, prompt-based blackbox optimization. QueryIPI leverages system invariants for initial seed generation aligned with agent conventions, and iterative reflection to resolve instruction-following failures and safety refusals. Experiments on five simulated agents show that QueryIPI achieves up to 87% success rate, outperforming the best baseline (50%). Crucially, generated malicious descriptions transfer to real-world coding agents, highlighting a practical security risk."},"title":{"value":"QueryIPI: Query-agnostic Indirect Prompt Injection on Coding Agents"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yuchong Xie","username":""},{"fullname":"Zesen Liu","username":""},{"fullname":"Mingyu Luo","username":"~Mingyu_Luo1"},{"fullname":"Zhixiang Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Kaikai Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Zongjie Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Ping Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Shuai Wang","username":""},{"fullname":"Dongdong She","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784717852664,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2510-23675"],"tcdate":1784717848527,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Mingyu_Luo1"],"forum":"kypuOFmQp4","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":90983,"cdate":1759276800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784717852664,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"kypuOFmQp4","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Pattern Recognit. 2026"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhao|structured_promptguided_knowledge_injection_for_medical_image_segmentation"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patcog.2026.113852"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/pr/ZhaoHZZZL26,\n  author={Yiyang Zhao and Kelei He and Yizhe Zhang and Yi Zhou and Tao Zhou and Dong Liang},\n  title={Structured prompt-guided knowledge injection for medical image segmentation},\n  year={2026},\n  cdate={1767225600000},\n  journal={Pattern Recognit.},\n  volume={179},\n  pages={113852},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patcog.2026.113852}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Structured prompt-guided knowledge injection for medical image segmentation"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yiyang Zhao","username":""},{"fullname":"Kelei He","username":""},{"fullname":"Yizhe Zhang","username":""},{"fullname":"Yi Zhou","username":"~Yi_Zhou8"},{"fullname":"Tao Zhou","username":""},{"fullname":"Dong Liang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1784682394923,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/pr/ZhaoHZZZL26"],"tcdate":1784682392488,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yi_Zhou8"],"forum":"icifwL5YkQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":86433,"cdate":1767225600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1784682394923,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"icifwL5YkQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"FAGEN@ICML 2026 Poster"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/8a79b05b484ed37956a99687870099cc6369f881.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM safety","Prompt injection"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|learning_to_inject_automated_prompt_injection_via_reinforcement_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xin_Chen15","~Jie_Zhang14","~Florian_Tramèr1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection is a critical vulnerability in LLM agents, yet the strongest methods still rely on human red-teamers and hand-crafted prompts. Adapting automated jailbreak optimizers does not close this gap: jailbreaks shape models toward generic compliance, while prompt injection requires emitting specific tool calls with correct parameters. The success signal is binary, and randomly sampled suffixes almost never trigger it—so standard optimizers have no gradient to follow. We present AutoInject, a black-box reinforcement learning (RL) framework that learns adversarial suffixes for prompt injection. A learned comparison-based reward scores each candidate against the best suffix seen so far, turning the binary signal into a dense reward suitable for RL optimization. The framework supports both online query-based attacks and offline-trained transferable suffixes that need no utility access at deployment, and incorporates a utility objective when task-completion feedback is available. On AgentDojo, AutoInject outperforms template attacks, GCG, TAP, and adaptive attack across production models, with statistically significant improvements under McNemar's test with $p<0.05$. Suffixes learned by AutoInject also break Meta-SecAlign-70B, a model fine-tuned specifically to resist prompt injection, where template attacks fail outright. The results establish an automated baseline for prompt injection and expose a gap between preference-based defenses and adaptive optimization-based attackers."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nchen2026learning,\ntitle={Learning to Inject: Automated Prompt Injection via Reinforcement Learning},\nauthor={Xin Chen and Jie Zhang and Florian Tram{\\`e}r},\nbooktitle={Workshop on Failure Modes of Agentic AI at ICML 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=0HxijK0AJZ}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Learning to Inject: Automated Prompt Injection via Reinforcement Learning"},"authors":{"value":["Xin Chen","Jie Zhang","Florian Tramèr"]}},"tmdate":1784105865690,"pdate":1780441951656,"tcdate":1778269564158,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/Submission101/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/Submission101/Authors"],"forum":"0HxijK0AJZ","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":101,"cdate":1778269564158,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/-/Post_Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN/Submission101/-/Revision"],"mdate":1784105865690,"odate":1780441951656,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/FAGEN","id":"0HxijK0AJZ","version":2},{"content":{"data_release":{"value":"We authorize the release of our submission and author names to the public in the event of acceptance."},"venue":{"value":"ICML 2026 AIWILD"},"email_sharing":{"value":"We authorize the sharing of all author emails with Program Chairs."},"keywords":{"value":["LLM safety","Prompt injection"]},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection is a critical vulnerability in LLM agents, yet the strongest methods still rely on human red-teamers and hand-crafted prompts. Adapting automated jailbreak optimizers does not close this gap: jailbreaks shape models toward generic compliance, while prompt injection requires emitting specific tool calls with correct parameters. The success signal is binary, and randomly sampled suffixes almost never trigger it—so standard optimizers have no gradient to follow. We present AutoInject, a black-box reinforcement learning (RL) framework that learns adversarial suffixes for prompt injection. A learned comparison-based reward scores each candidate against the best suffix seen so far, turning the binary signal into a dense reward suitable for RL optimization. The framework supports both online query-based attacks and offline-trained transferable suffixes that need no utility access at deployment, and incorporates a utility objective when task-completion feedback is available. On AgentDojo, AutoInject outperforms template attacks, GCG, TAP, and adaptive attack across production models, with statistically significant improvements under McNemar's test with $p<0.05$. Suffixes learned by AutoInject also break Meta-SecAlign-70B, a model fine-tuned specifically to resist prompt injection, where template attacks fail outright. The results establish an automated baseline for prompt injection and expose a gap between preference-based defenses and adaptive optimization-based attackers."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nchen2026learning,\ntitle={Learning to Inject: Automated Prompt Injection via Reinforcement Learning},\nauthor={Xin Chen and Jie Zhang and Florian Tram{\\`e}r},\nbooktitle={Second Workshop on Agents in the Wild: Safety, Security, and Beyond},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=ILFICFzCio}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Learning to Inject: Automated Prompt Injection via Reinforcement Learning"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9d4f096d2bf535556158932357a845983c6e438f.pdf","readers":["everyone"]},"venueid":{"value":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|learning_to_inject_automated_prompt_injection_via_reinforcement_learning"},"authorids":{"value":["~Xin_Chen15","~Jie_Zhang14","~Florian_Tramèr1"]},"track":{"value":"Regular Paper (9 pages)"},"authors":{"value":["Xin Chen","Jie Zhang","Florian Tramèr"]}},"tmdate":1783293491771,"pdate":1779518192432,"tcdate":1778269488585,"writers":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/Submission187/Authors"],"signatures":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/Submission187/Authors"],"forum":"ILFICFzCio","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":187,"cdate":1778269488585,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Submission","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Edit","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/-/Submission_Release","ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD/Submission187/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1783293491771,"odate":1779518192432,"domain":"ICML.cc/2026/Workshop/AIWILD","id":"ILFICFzCio","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Prompt Injection Attack, AI Security, Large Language Models"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2024 October Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025defense,\ntitle={Defense Against Prompt Injection Attack by Leveraging Attack Techniques},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - October 2024},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Olqv1AJsc1},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Defense Against Prompt Injection Attack by Leveraging Attack Techniques"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment","Approaches to low-resource settings"]},"abstract":{"value":"With the advancement of technology, large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable performance across various natural language processing (NLP) tasks, powering LLM-integrated applications like Microsoft Copilot. However, as LLMs continue to evolve, new vulnerabilities, especially prompt injection attacks arise. These attacks trick LLMs into deviating from the original input instructions and executing the attacker's instructions injected in data content, such as retrieved results. Recent attack methods leverage LLMs’ instruction-following abilities and their inabilities to distinguish instructions injected in the data content, and achieve a high attack success rate (ASR). When comparing the attack and defense methods, we interestingly find that they share similar design goals, of inducing the model to ignore unwanted instructions and instead to execute wanted instructions. Therefore, we raise an intuitive question: *Could these attack techniques be utilized for defensive purposes?* In this paper, we invert the intention of prompt injection methods to develop novel defense methods based on previous training-free attack methods, by repeating the attack process but with the original input instruction rather than the injected instruction. Our comprehensive experiments demonstrate that our defense techniques outperform existing training-free defense approaches, achieving state-of-the-art results."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/c64fe9ac1ee6c0fef1acaec87055ce8cb89044dd.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"NLP Applications"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962348851,"tcdate":1729007516961,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October/Submission1171/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October/Submission1171/Authors"],"forum":"Olqv1AJsc1","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":1171,"cdate":1729007516961,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962348851,"odate":1736460501709,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2024/October","id":"Olqv1AJsc1","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"adversarial machine learning; security and privacy; red teaming;"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2025 May Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2025transferable,\ntitle={Transferable Direct Prompt Injection via Activation-Guided {MCMC} Sampling},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2025},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=eSA58GC7bc},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Transferable Direct Prompt Injection via Activation-Guided MCMC Sampling"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment"]},"abstract":{"value":"Direct Prompt Injection (DPI) attacks pose a critical security threat to Large Language Models (LLMs) due to their low barrier of execution and high potential damage. \nTo address the impracticality of existing white-box/gray-box methods and the poor transferability of black-box methods, we propose an activations-guided prompt injection attack framework. We first construct an Energy-based Model (EBM) using activations from a surrogate model to evaluate the quality of adversarial prompts. Guided by the trained EBM, we employ the token-level Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling to adaptively optimize adversarial prompts, thereby enabling gradient-free black-box attacks. \nExperimental results demonstrate our superior cross-model transferability, achieving 49.6% attack success rate (ASR) across five mainstream LLMs and 34.6% improvement over human-crafted prompts, and maintaining 36.6% ASR on unseen task scenarios. \nInterpretability analysis reveals a correlation between activations and attack effectiveness, highlighting the critical role of semantic patterns in transferable vulnerability exploitation."},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/7b44f7fef928435ff02a6ca55571bdbb966c2885.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Language Modeling"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1782962090130,"tcdate":1747642192050,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission2685/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/Submission2685/Authors"],"forum":"eSA58GC7bc","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":2685,"cdate":1747642192050,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1782962090130,"odate":1753766020949,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2025/May","id":"eSA58GC7bc","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"EMNLP 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.372.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|topicattack_an_indirect_prompt_injection_attack_via_topic_transition"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.372"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/emnlp/ChenLLLSH25,\n  author={Yulin Chen and Haoran Li and Yuexin Li and Yue Liu and Yangqiu Song and Bryan Hooi},\n  title={TopicAttack: An Indirect Prompt Injection Attack via Topic Transition},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={7327-7345},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.372},\n  booktitle={EMNLP},\n  crossref={conf/emnlp/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown remarkable performance across a range of NLP tasks. However, their strong instruction-following capabilities and inability to distinguish instructions from data content make them vulnerable to indirect prompt injection attacks. In such attacks, instructions with malicious purposes are injected into external data sources, such as web documents. When LLMs retrieve this injected data through tools, such as a search engine and execute the injected instructions, they provide misled responses. Recent attack methods have demonstrated potential, but their abrupt instruction injection often undermines their effectiveness. Motivated by the limitations of existing attack methods, we propose **TopicAttack**, which prompts the LLM to generate a fabricated conversational transition prompt that gradually shifts the topic toward the injected instruction, making the injection smoother and enhancing the plausibility and success of the attack. Through comprehensive experiments, TopicAttack achieves state-of-the-art performance, with an attack success rate (ASR) over 90% in most cases, even when various defense methods are applied. We further analyze its effectiveness by examining attention scores. We find that a higher injected-to-original attention ratio leads to a greater success probability, and our method achieves a much higher ratio than the baseline methods."},"title":{"value":"TopicAttack: An Indirect Prompt Injection Attack via Topic Transition"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yulin Chen","username":""},{"fullname":"Haoran Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Yuexin Li","username":"~Yuexin_Li1"},{"fullname":"Yue Liu","username":""},{"fullname":"Yangqiu Song","username":""},{"fullname":"Bryan Hooi","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1782195148475,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/emnlp/ChenLLLSH25"],"tcdate":1782195145486,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yuexin_Li1"],"forum":"ygYmOLKgot","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":46209,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1782195148475,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"ygYmOLKgot","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"Language Modeling,NLP Applications,"},"keywords":{"value":["robustness","defense","security","injection","reasoning","secure","training","alignment","agent"]},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 January Submission"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026nonreasoning,\ntitle={Non-Reasoning transfers to Reasoning: Agentic Prompt Injection Defense},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - January 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=XyReWTxywx},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Non-Reasoning transfers to Reasoning: Agentic Prompt Injection Defense"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment"]},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injections are a critical issue limiting adoption of LLMs for interacting with insecure data. This particularly limits the ability of agents interacting with the outside world. We combat this limitation by introducing Reasoning SecAlign, a training approach specifically targeted at training robustness into reasoning LLMs.\nBy leveraging the connection between reasoning and non-reasoning mode, we are able to harden reasoning LLMs by training on their non-reasoning distribution.\nTraining based interventions incur no inference time overhead compared to test time scaling and have efficiency and flexibility improvements over system based methods. We maintain benchmark utility across a wide range of evaluations, and reduce indirect prompt injection attack success rates to 0 or near 0."},"paper_type":{"value":"Short"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6662b5f938a83078d5c237e920446c178dd15ad5.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Safety and Alignment in LLMs"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission"}},"tmdate":1780805086761,"tcdate":1767601681966,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission4611/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/Submission4611/Authors"],"forum":"XyReWTxywx","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":4611,"cdate":1767601681966,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Preprint_Release_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Desk_Reject_Verification_Flag","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January/-/Preprint_Post_Submission"],"mdate":1780805086761,"odate":1774007940523,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/January","id":"XyReWTxywx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Crossref"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"jaiswal|analysis_of_llms_against_prompt_injection_and_jailbreak_attacks"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3803628.3807972"},"title":{"value":"Analysis of LLMs Against Prompt Injection and Jailbreak Attacks"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Piyush Jaiswal","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Piyush%20Jaiswal"},{"fullname":"Aaditya Pratap","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Aaditya%20Pratap"},{"fullname":"Shreyansh Saraswati","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Shreyansh%20Saraswati"},{"fullname":"Harsh Kasyap","username":"~Harsh_Kasyap2"},{"fullname":"Somanath Tripathy","username":"https://orcid.org/orcid-search/search?searchQuery=Somanath%20Tripathy"}]}},"tmdate":1779876191098,"pdate":1780272000000,"externalIds":["doi:10.1145/3803628.3807972"],"tcdate":1779876189716,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Harsh_Kasyap2"],"forum":"S1OgYrXxHx","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":73898,"cdate":1779259848120,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/ORCID.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1779876191098,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"S1OgYrXxHx","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"jaiswal|analysis_of_llms_against_prompt_injection_and_jailbreak_attacks"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.22242"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2602-22242,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Piyush Jaiswal and Aaditya Pratap and Shreyansh Saraswati and Harsh Kasyap and Somanath Tripathy},\n  title={Analysis of LLMs Against Prompt Injection and Jailbreak Attacks},\n  year={2026},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1769904000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2602.22242},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.22242}\n}\n"},"title":{"value":"Analysis of LLMs Against Prompt Injection and Jailbreak Attacks"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Piyush Jaiswal","username":""},{"fullname":"Aaditya Pratap","username":""},{"fullname":"Shreyansh Saraswati","username":""},{"fullname":"Harsh Kasyap","username":"~Harsh_Kasyap2"},{"fullname":"Somanath Tripathy","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1779876135091,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2602-22242"],"tcdate":1779876134717,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Harsh_Kasyap2"],"forum":"RZQI3frKmt","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":33907,"cdate":1769904000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1779876135091,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"RZQI3frKmt","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 Datasets and Benchmarks Track poster"},"keywords":{"value":["agents","security","prompt injections","web agents"]},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nevtimov2026wasp,\ntitle={{WASP}: Benchmarking Web Agent Security Against Prompt Injection Attacks},\nauthor={Ivan Evtimov and Arman Zharmagambetov and Aaron Grattafiori and Chuan Guo and Kamalika Chaudhuri},\nbooktitle={The Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems Datasets and Benchmarks Track},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=Ip1cCUAllL}\n}"},"title":{"value":"WASP: Benchmarking Web Agent Security Against Prompt Injection Attacks"},"paperhash":{"value":"evtimov|wasp_benchmarking_web_agent_security_against_prompt_injection_attacks"},"dataset_URL":{"value":"https://github.com/facebookresearch/wasp/tree/main/webarena_prompt_injections/configs/croissant"},"TLDR":{"value":"A benchmark with realistic security scenarios for web agents based on LLMs"},"primary_area":{"value":"social_and_economic_aspects_of_datasets_and_benchmarks_in_machine_learning"},"flagged_for_ethics_review":{"value":true},"abstract":{"value":"Autonomous UI agents powered by AI have tremendous potential to boost human productivity by automating routine tasks such as filing taxes and paying bills. However, a major challenge in unlocking their full potential is security, which is exacerbated by the agent's ability to take action on their user's behalf. Existing tests for prompt injections in web agents either over-simplify the threat by testing unrealistic scenarios or giving the attacker too much power, or look at single-step isolated tasks. To more accurately measure progress for secure web agents, we introduce WASP – a new publicly available benchmark for end-to-end evaluation of Web Agent Security against Prompt Injection attacks. Evaluating with WASP shows that even top-tier AI models, including those with advanced reasoning capabilities, can be deceived by simple, low-effort human-written injections in very realistic scenarios. Our end-to-end evaluation reveals a previously unobserved insight: while attacks partially succeed in up to 86% of the case, even state-of-the-art agents often struggle to fully complete the attacker goals – highlighting the current state of security by incompetence. Code and data are available at https://github.com/facebookresearch/wasp."},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/9f0e2f0e31feb185a69a36088690e7cc037e71bc.pdf"},"croissant_file":{"value":"/attachment/afda31cd59bd2423e0ac5cf226286a95891b3a59.json"},"code_URL":{"value":"https://github.com/facebookresearch/wasp"},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track"},"authorids":{"value":["~Ivan_Evtimov2","~Arman_Zharmagambetov1","~Aaron_Grattafiori1","~Chuan_Guo1","~Kamalika_Chaudhuri1"]},"authors":{"value":["Ivan Evtimov","Arman Zharmagambetov","Aaron Grattafiori","Chuan Guo","Kamalika Chaudhuri"]}},"tmdate":1776955793314,"pdate":1758215769005,"tcdate":1746791260601,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission945/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission945/Authors"],"forum":"Ip1cCUAllL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":945,"cdate":1746791260601,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission945/-/Full_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/-/Ethics_Review_Flag","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track/Submission945/-/Camera_Ready_Revision"],"mdate":1776955793314,"odate":1761793847640,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track","id":"Ip1cCUAllL","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.19793v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"shi|prompt_injection_attack_to_tool_selection_in_llm_agents"},"authorids":{"value":["~Jiawen_Shi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zenghui_Yuan:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guiyao_Tie:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pan_Zhou_0001:","~Neil_Zhenqiang_Gong1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Lichao_Sun_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.19793"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2504-19793,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Jiawen Shi and Zenghui Yuan and Guiyao Tie and Pan Zhou and Neil Zhenqiang Gong and Lichao Sun},\n  title={Prompt Injection Attack to Tool Selection in LLM Agents},\n  year={2025},\n  month={April},\n  cdate={1743465600000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2504.19793},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.19793}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Tool selection is a key component of LLM agents. A popular approach follows a two-step process - \\emph{retrieval} and \\emph{selection} - to pick the most appropriate tool from a tool library for a given task. In this work, we introduce \\textit{ToolHijacker}, a novel prompt injection attack targeting tool selection in no-box scenarios. ToolHijacker injects a malicious tool document into the tool library to manipulate the LLM agent's tool selection process, compelling it to consistently choose the attacker's malicious tool for an attacker-chosen target task. Specifically, we formulate the crafting of such tool documents as an optimization problem and propose a two-phase optimization strategy to solve it. Our extensive experimental evaluation shows that ToolHijacker is highly effective, significantly outperforming existing manual-based and automated prompt injection attacks when applied to tool selection. Moreover, we explore various defenses, including prevention-based defenses (StruQ and SecAlign) and detection-based defenses (known-answer detection, DataSentinel, perplexity detection, and perplexity windowed detection). Our experimental results indicate that these defenses are insufficient, highlighting the urgent need for developing new defense strategies."},"title":{"value":"Prompt Injection Attack to Tool Selection in LLM Agents"},"authors":{"value":["Jiawen Shi","Zenghui Yuan","Guiyao Tie","Pan Zhou","Neil Zhenqiang Gong","Lichao Sun"]}},"tmdate":1775040097246,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2504-19793"],"tcdate":1764166584269,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Neil_Gong2"],"forum":"cYpWByKDib","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":706379,"cdate":1743465600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1775040097246,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"cYpWByKDib","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"},"keywords":{"value":["prompt injection defense","intent analysis","reasoning model"]},"primary_area":{"value":"alignment, fairness, safety, privacy, and societal considerations"},"abstract":{"value":"Indirect prompt injection attacks (IPIAs), where large language models (LLMs) follow malicious instructions hidden in input data, pose a critical threat to LLM-powered agents. In this paper, we present IntentGuard, a general defense framework based on instruction-following intent analysis. The key insight of IntentGuard is that the decisive factor in IPIAs is not the presence of malicious text, but whether the LLM intends to follow instructions from untrusted data. Building on this insight, IntentGuard leverages an instruction-following intent analyzer (IIA) to identify which parts of the input prompt the model recognizes as actionable instructions, and then flag or neutralize any overlaps with untrusted data segments. To instantiate the framework, we develop an IIA that uses three ``thinking intervention'' strategies to elicit a structured list of intended instructions from reasoning-enabled LLMs. These techniques include start-of-thinking prefilling, end-of-thinking refinement, and adversarial in-context demonstration. We evaluate IntentGuard on two agentic benchmarks (AgentDojo and Mind2Web) using two reasoning-enabled LLMs (Qwen-3-32B and gpt-oss-20B). Results demonstrate that IntentGuard achieves (1) no utility degradation in {all but one setting} and (2) strong robustness against adaptive prompt injection attacks (e.g., reducing attack success rates from 100\\% to 8.5\\% in a Mind2Web scenario)."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@misc{\nkang2026mitigating,\ntitle={Mitigating Indirect Prompt Injection via Instruction-Following Intent Analysis},\nauthor={Mintong Kang and Chong Xiang and Sanjay Kariyappa and Chaowei Xiao and Bo Li and G. Edward Suh},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=fF9alVesJ0}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Mitigating Indirect Prompt Injection via Instruction-Following Intent Analysis"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/388a10bea60cf6a877868789cca682ccf22cd6a4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Rejected_Submission"},"paperhash":{"value":"kang|mitigating_indirect_prompt_injection_via_instructionfollowing_intent_analysis"},"authorids":{"value":["~Mintong_Kang1","~Chong_Xiang1","~Sanjay_Kariyappa1","~Chaowei_Xiao2","~Bo_Li19","~G._Edward_Suh2"]},"authors":{"value":["Mintong Kang","Chong Xiang","Sanjay Kariyappa","Chaowei Xiao","Bo Li","G. Edward Suh"]}},"tmdate":1770805165075,"tcdate":1758334452064,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission22686/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission22686/Authors"],"forum":"fF9alVesJ0","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":22686,"cdate":1758334452064,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Post_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/Submission22686/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2026/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1770805165075,"odate":1759896705795,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2026/Conference","id":"fF9alVesJ0","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.00459v3"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|defense_against_prompt_injection_attack_by_leveraging_attack_techniques"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yulin_Chen3","~Haoran_Li3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Zihao_Zheng:","~Yangqiu_Song1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dekai_Wu:","~Bryan_Hooi1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.00459"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2411-00459,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Yulin Chen and Haoran Li and Zihao Zheng and Yangqiu Song and Dekai Wu and Bryan Hooi},\n  title={Defense Against Prompt Injection Attack by Leveraging Attack Techniques},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2411.00459},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2411.00459}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"With the advancement of technology, large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable performance across various natural language processing (NLP) tasks, powering LLM-integrated applications like Microsoft Copilot. However, as LLMs continue to evolve, new vulnerabilities, especially prompt injection attacks arise. These attacks trick LLMs into deviating from the original input instructions and executing the attacker's instructions injected in data content, such as retrieved results. Recent attack methods leverage LLMs' instruction-following abilities and their inabilities to distinguish instructions injected in the data content, and achieve a high attack success rate (ASR). When comparing the attack and defense methods, we interestingly find that they share similar design goals, of inducing the model to ignore unwanted instructions and instead to execute wanted instructions. Therefore, we raise an intuitive question: Could these attack techniques be utilized for defensive purposes? In this paper, we invert the intention of prompt injection methods to develop novel defense methods based on previous training-free attack methods, by repeating the attack process but with the original input instruction rather than the injected instruction. Our comprehensive experiments demonstrate that our defense techniques outperform existing training-free defense approaches, achieving state-of-the-art results."},"title":{"value":"Defense Against Prompt Injection Attack by Leveraging Attack Techniques"},"authors":{"value":["Yulin Chen","Haoran Li","Zihao Zheng","Yangqiu Song","Dekai Wu","Bryan Hooi"]}},"tmdate":1769323279473,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1747396109620,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yangqiu_Song1"],"forum":"DB7z24cjUT","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":513104,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769323279473,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"DB7z24cjUT","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (1) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.890.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ACL/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|can_indirect_prompt_injection_attacks_be_detected_and_removed"},"authorids":{"value":["~Yulin_Chen3","~Haoran_Li3","~Yuan_Sui1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yufei_He:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yue_Liu_0008:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yangqiu_Song:","~Bryan_Hooi1"]},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.890/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/ChenLSH0SH25,\n  author={Yulin Chen and Haoran Li and Yuan Sui and Yufei He and Yue Liu and Yangqiu Song and Bryan Hooi},\n  title={Can Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks Be Detected and Removed?},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={18189-18206},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.890/},\n  booktitle={ACL (1)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2025-1}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Prompt injection attacks manipulate large language models (LLMs) by misleading them to deviate from the original input instructions and execute maliciously injected instructions, because of their instruction-following capabilities and inability to distinguish between the original input instructions and maliciously injected instructions. To defend against such attacks, recent studies have developed various detection mechanisms. If we restrict ourselves specifically to works which perform detection rather than direct defense, most of them focus on direct prompt injection attacks, while there are few works for the indirect scenario, where injected instructions are indirectly from external tools, such as a search engine. Moreover, current works mainly investigate injection detection methods and pay less attention to the post-processing method that aims to mitigate the injection after detection.In this paper, we investigate the feasibility of detecting and removing indirect prompt injection attacks, and we construct a benchmark dataset for evaluation. For detection, we assess the performance of existing LLMs and open-source detection models, and we further train detection models using our crafted training datasets. For removal, we evaluate two intuitive methods: (1) the *segmentation removal method*, which segments the injected document and removes parts containing injected instructions, and (2) the *extraction removal method*, which trains an extraction model to identify and remove injected instructions."},"title":{"value":"Can Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks Be Detected and Removed?"},"authors":{"value":["Yulin Chen","Haoran Li","Yuan Sui","Yufei He","Yue Liu","Yangqiu Song","Bryan Hooi"]}},"tmdate":1769323279463,"pdate":1735689600000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/ChenLSH0SH25"],"tcdate":1754072886670,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yuan_Sui1"],"forum":"P8vKRGBWhr","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":609741,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1769323279463,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"P8vKRGBWhr","version":2}],"count":8798}